window.CET6_BANKS.push({"id": "2025-12-1", "source": "2025年12月真题·第1套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay that begins with the sentence \"While striving for the Chinese Dream, young people enjoy more opportunities to realize their self-worth.\" You can make comments, cite examples or use your personal experiences to develop your essay. You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words. You should copy the sentence given in quotes at the beginning of your essay.", "sample": "While striving for the Chinese Dream, young people enjoy more opportunities to realize their self-worth. As\nthe nation advances, doors that were once closed are swinging open for the younger generation. Specifically,\nthe rapid growth of technology, education and entrepreneurship offers a broad stage on which youth can turn\ntheir talents into real contributions.\nThese opportunities become meaningful only when young people seize them actively. Take my cousin as an\nexample: instead of chasing a comfortable office job, she founded a start-up that brings online courses to rural\nchildren. For instance, thousands of students now learn from teachers they could never have met, and she has\nfound genuine value in her work. In this way, personal ambition and national progress reinforce each other.\nIn a word, the Chinese Dream is not a distant slogan but a living platform that lets every young person shine.\nAs long as we combine individual effort with the tide of the times, we can both realize our self-worth and push\nthe country forward."}, "translation": {"prompt": "尊老是中华民族的传统美德，深深植根于中国人的思想和行为中，是人们普遍遵守的行为规范和社会准则。在当今的中国，这种美德得到广泛传承。社会各界积极营造敬老助老的社会氛围。为老年人提供便利服务已成为社会共识，例如社区专门开设长者食堂，公共场所配置优先座位。政府还出台了一系列政策，为老年人的权益提供有力保障。尊老是社会和谐与发展的重要基石，有助于培育良好的社会氛围，推动整个社会文明进步。", "sample": "Respecting the elderly is a traditional virtue of the Chinese nation. Deeply rooted in the thoughts and behavior of the Chinese people, it is a code of conduct and a social norm that people widely observe. In China today, this virtue is widely carried forward. People from all walks of life actively foster a social atmosphere of respecting and helping the elderly. Providing convenient services for the elderly has become a social consensus. For example, communities open special canteens for the elderly, and public places provide priority seats. The government has also introduced a series of policies, providing strong protection for the rights and interests of the elderly. Respecting the elderly is an important cornerstone of social harmony and development, helping to foster a sound social atmosphere and promote the civilized progress of the whole society."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet6/paper/2025-12-1/?f=w", "clozePassage": "By now, it is generally accepted that regular exercise comes with an assortment of health benefits for people who maintain the habit over time. But is it  __26__  too late to start?\nNew research looked at people who increased their exercise levels after their 40s beyond levels they had when they were younger and those individuals showed declines in their risk of early  __27__  which were similar to those of people who exercised consistently throughout their lives. This was a  __28__  of 32% to 35% compared to people who did not exercise. Those who began exercising in middle age also saw reductions in their incidence of  __29__  diseases, such as heart disease and cancer, which were similar to the steady exercisers.\nSo why bother exercising consistently throughout your life? The authors of the study  __30__  that some might interpret the study's findings to mean that they can delay starting an exercise program but this would be a mistake. There are other benefits, both mental and physical, to staying consistently active that we should not  __31__  . These real health benefits include better  __32__  functioning and lowered risk of gaining weight. The latter is  __33__  important given the global obesity epidemic. In addition, experts assert that starting younger is better because, if you make a habit of exercising when you are young, you are more likely to keep up those  __34__  later in life. It is ideal, then, for people to start exercising when they are children or  __35__  , and it is good to maintain an active lifestyle at all times regardless of your age. However, this study does indicate that, if you have not been active, you can still benefit if you start becoming active in your 40s and 50s.", "matchIntro": "Restaurants Are Now Employing Robots — Should Chefs Be Worried?", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "Like most chefs, Flippy is not afraid of hard work. Since last summer, that has meant 11 am until 7 pm shifts at Caliburger in Pasadena, California, as well as short assignments at Chick-N-Tots at Dodger Stadium in Los Angeles. These are long hours of repetitive work, where the boss demands absolute consistency. But you won't hear Flippy complain or say anything, in fact. For Flippy is a robot — a cloud-connected mechanical arm with 3D thermal scanners for eyes — that can flip burgers or fry 80 baskets of food an hour, monitor that food and even clean up afterwards."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Flippy was created by Miso Robotics, part of the Cali Group, which is described by its CEO, John Miller, as: \"A technology company that happens to sell cheeseburgers.\" Cali creates new machines that it road-tests in its Caliburger restaurants across the world, into which Flippy will be deployed this year. And Flippy is not alone. Also in California, Bear Robotics has developed a self-guiding robot, Penny, which has so far served 40,000 diners."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Ten years ago, robot-chefs and waiters were pure science fiction. Today, they are a reality, and at prices that make them a plausible investment. From May, Penny will be shipped on a subscription basis that offers \"an immediate return\", says Bear Robotics' chief operating officer, Juan Higueros. Flippy will cost a reported $60,000 to $100,000. Fully automated burger restaurants managed with minimal human oversight — where customers order at screens, pay electronically, and eat food cooked and delivered by robots — are now a possibility."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The technology exists; it just needs knitting together cost-effectively and in a way diners buy into. In an industry keen to slash labor costs and increase profits, further automation seems inevitable. After an eight-month test at Kang Nam Tofu House in California, Bear Robotics credited Penny with driving a 28% increase in sales. Meanwhile, in US trials, the self-ordering screens that McDonald's is currently installing at its 1,300 UK restaurants yielded a reported 30% rise in order values."}, {"label": "E", "text": "Personalisation is seen as key to the appeal of this technology, alongside its ability to reliably push so-called \"upsells\" (meal deals, extras, larger drinks) to customers. Soon when you log into a restaurant app, it will be able to use your data and purchasing history to recommend dishes to you — factoring in everything from the weather to, if you are ordering at a screen that can read your face, your mood."}, {"label": "F", "text": "\"Right now people compromise on choice. We're presented with a one-size-fits-all menu. Ultimately, technology can allow us to make personal choices. You love spicy food and chicken? Here's the dish people like you mostly order,\" says Tom Weaver, CEO at the hospitality tech company Flyt. He talks enthusiastically about the Helsinki pop-up Take-In, a full-service restaurant where diners ordered dishes in from various delivery services. \"For the first time in a few hundred years, digital is allowing us to create new versions of restaurants.\""}, {"label": "G", "text": "If high-street fast food is automation-ready, its impact on high-end restaurants is likely to be subtler and slower. \"It'll probably be a very long time before we see a five-star robot-chef,\" says Noel Sharkey, professor of AI and robotics at the University of Sheffield. \"Apart from the AI creativity gap, great cooking involves a subtle understanding of ingredients and delicate cooking that would be enormously challenging for robots. Placing fragile foods on plates would be incredibly slow.\""}, {"label": "H", "text": "Currently, robots have limited functionality. A human needs to load Penny with plates that diners lift off themselves. At the robot-powered US pizza-delivery company Zume, the more difficult jobs, such as topping pizzas, are still done by humans. But Moley, which will launch soon, offers an idea of the sophistication to come. Modelled on the movements of a MasterChef contest winner, Tim Anderson, its robotic arms are fixed over a stove and programmed to prepare dishes from raw ingredients. It is designed for domestic use, but a commercial version is planned that opens up the possibility of restaurant kitchens automating even highly technically challenging tasks."}, {"label": "I", "text": "For now, the kitchen technology that QSR Automations supplies to Mitchells & Butlers' pubs or KFC is less glamorous. Rather than having a chef shouting out diners' orders, the QSR system automatically distributes those orders across screens. The kitchen screens then tell chefs when to start cooking each dish, so that your plate of fish, chips and peas comes together at the right time."}, {"label": "J", "text": "The restaurant industry is currently suffering a severe shortage of skilled chefs — a deficit of 11,000, according to the skills agency People 1st. \"Automation systems designed to make the process more idiot-proof are trying to bridge that gap,\" says Stefan Chomka, the editor of Restaurant magazine."}, {"label": "K", "text": "In terms of simplifying or enhancing the customer experience, online restaurant booking has been the easily-won success of the internet age. Automated at-table ordering and payment is seen as the next landmark. Until then, early adopters, such as the Manchester bar owner Andy Smith, who uses the Ordoo app at his venue 33 Oldham Street, may well experience growing but \"lower than expected\" acceptance."}, {"label": "L", "text": "Embarrassing failures in restaurant tech frequently hit the headlines. The TGI Fridays drone(无人机) that injured a Brooklyn Daily photographer; US burger chain Shake Shack having to retreat from plans for entirely cashless ordering sites. But, says Chomka, such technology could miss the mark if it only offers \"solutions to problems that don't really exist\". \"Payments being automatic\" could be useful, he adds. But, in an expensive restaurant, \"how quick do you need dinner to be?\""}, {"label": "M", "text": "That attachment to the human art of hospitality will be heartening for staff who see automation as a threat to jobs. \"Unite is concerned that without safeguards, any benefits will bypass workers in low-paying jobs, such as waiters,\" says the union's London regional officer, Dave Turnbull. \"We're urging Members of Parliament to ensure automation does not just benefit a wealthy few like previous industrial revolutions.\""}, {"label": "N", "text": "The industry principle is that automation will allow it to redeploy staff more effectively. Jobs will change, if not immediately decrease in number. \"Self-order screens mean more demand for people front-of-house. Last year, we recruited 1,000 new managers,\" says McDonald's COO, Jason Clark. Caliburger has a programme to retrain staff as robot engineers or \"chef techs\"."}, {"label": "O", "text": "But ultimately the chance to reduce overheads(管理费用) by employing fewer people or at lower rates will be what attracts big brands to technology. Invariably, over the next 10 years, jobs will go in conventional bricks-built restaurants, not least because automation will boost their rapidly growing rival: delivery. Companies such as Domino and Just Eat have both experimented with robot and drone delivery, which is already legal in Reykjavik, Iceland. \"Robot food deliveries are the future,\" says Noel Sharkey. \"Small, 4 mph ground robots are safe and will work 24 hours a day.\""}], "readingPassages": {"46": "Many see friendships as a comfort blanket: a shoulder to cry on, a reliable soul to confide in. However, more often than not, in our eagerness to seek individual success, we lose friends along the way and that has become part of the process.\nThe chaos and never-ending turmoil that accompanies everyday life is an aggressive obstacle in the pursuit of stable friendships. As students, we use this excuse to shrug off losing people who we once thought to be loved ones.\nInstead of viewing our lives as a race, where everyone is on their own path to some form of success, we must relearn to value the connections we make along the track. We must reevaluate the harm of radical, individual freedom.\nRadical individualism elevates the freedom of the individual over the collective. It argues, for instance, that universal healthcare, an example of collectivism, threatened the dream of many Americans.\nMany of us have fallen victim to an individualistic culture. Society tends to celebrate personal achievement and commend it based on the assumption that winning or succeeding is a consequence of working harder than the next person.\nAs a society, we idolize the concept of \"losing friends on the road to success\". I remember hearing in high school, \"Don't worry about losing friendships, just focus on study and getting into college. That's all that will matter in the long run,\" a sentiment that is not unique to my experience. It's preached in many success stories, and it's embedded in the way we see growth. Motivational speakers will push their audience to be complacent to the inevitable end of losing friends. Spend too much time with friends? You're losing time better utilized on your achievements. Invest time in your relationships? Invest time in your work instead.\nThis constant need to succeed has blinded us from seeing through the whirlwind of change that accompanies our everyday lives. We are expected to tangibly succeed, earn awards, get higher positions, earn better grades. In order to succeed, we lose friends, and that has sadly become normalized.\nWe cannot be complacent with losing friendships because they are our anchors. Yes, pursue success. Challenge yourself. Push limits. But contrary to all the self-proclaimed success experts, doing it alone does not make you more successful or any better than someone who achieved it with the help of others.\nPut effort into fostering healthy relationships, and maintaining them through your busy schedule that seems like it leaves no time for anyone else. Although we may be living in a society that treasures the independent pursuit of achievement, having people to support and love you on the way is far more important than any end result.", "47": "Many see friendships as a comfort blanket: a shoulder to cry on, a reliable soul to confide in. However, more often than not, in our eagerness to seek individual success, we lose friends along the way and that has become part of the process.\nThe chaos and never-ending turmoil that accompanies everyday life is an aggressive obstacle in the pursuit of stable friendships. As students, we use this excuse to shrug off losing people who we once thought to be loved ones.\nInstead of viewing our lives as a race, where everyone is on their own path to some form of success, we must relearn to value the connections we make along the track. We must reevaluate the harm of radical, individual freedom.\nRadical individualism elevates the freedom of the individual over the collective. It argues, for instance, that universal healthcare, an example of collectivism, threatened the dream of many Americans.\nMany of us have fallen victim to an individualistic culture. Society tends to celebrate personal achievement and commend it based on the assumption that winning or succeeding is a consequence of working harder than the next person.\nAs a society, we idolize the concept of \"losing friends on the road to success\". I remember hearing in high school, \"Don't worry about losing friendships, just focus on study and getting into college. That's all that will matter in the long run,\" a sentiment that is not unique to my experience. It's preached in many success stories, and it's embedded in the way we see growth. Motivational speakers will push their audience to be complacent to the inevitable end of losing friends. Spend too much time with friends? You're losing time better utilized on your achievements. Invest time in your relationships? Invest time in your work instead.\nThis constant need to succeed has blinded us from seeing through the whirlwind of change that accompanies our everyday lives. We are expected to tangibly succeed, earn awards, get higher positions, earn better grades. In order to succeed, we lose friends, and that has sadly become normalized.\nWe cannot be complacent with losing friendships because they are our anchors. Yes, pursue success. Challenge yourself. Push limits. But contrary to all the self-proclaimed success experts, doing it alone does not make you more successful or any better than someone who achieved it with the help of others.\nPut effort into fostering healthy relationships, and maintaining them through your busy schedule that seems like it leaves no time for anyone else. Although we may be living in a society that treasures the independent pursuit of achievement, having people to support and love you on the way is far more important than any end result.", "48": "Many see friendships as a comfort blanket: a shoulder to cry on, a reliable soul to confide in. However, more often than not, in our eagerness to seek individual success, we lose friends along the way and that has become part of the process.\nThe chaos and never-ending turmoil that accompanies everyday life is an aggressive obstacle in the pursuit of stable friendships. As students, we use this excuse to shrug off losing people who we once thought to be loved ones.\nInstead of viewing our lives as a race, where everyone is on their own path to some form of success, we must relearn to value the connections we make along the track. We must reevaluate the harm of radical, individual freedom.\nRadical individualism elevates the freedom of the individual over the collective. It argues, for instance, that universal healthcare, an example of collectivism, threatened the dream of many Americans.\nMany of us have fallen victim to an individualistic culture. Society tends to celebrate personal achievement and commend it based on the assumption that winning or succeeding is a consequence of working harder than the next person.\nAs a society, we idolize the concept of \"losing friends on the road to success\". I remember hearing in high school, \"Don't worry about losing friendships, just focus on study and getting into college. That's all that will matter in the long run,\" a sentiment that is not unique to my experience. It's preached in many success stories, and it's embedded in the way we see growth. Motivational speakers will push their audience to be complacent to the inevitable end of losing friends. Spend too much time with friends? You're losing time better utilized on your achievements. Invest time in your relationships? Invest time in your work instead.\nThis constant need to succeed has blinded us from seeing through the whirlwind of change that accompanies our everyday lives. We are expected to tangibly succeed, earn awards, get higher positions, earn better grades. In order to succeed, we lose friends, and that has sadly become normalized.\nWe cannot be complacent with losing friendships because they are our anchors. Yes, pursue success. Challenge yourself. Push limits. But contrary to all the self-proclaimed success experts, doing it alone does not make you more successful or any better than someone who achieved it with the help of others.\nPut effort into fostering healthy relationships, and maintaining them through your busy schedule that seems like it leaves no time for anyone else. Although we may be living in a society that treasures the independent pursuit of achievement, having people to support and love you on the way is far more important than any end result.", "49": "Many see friendships as a comfort blanket: a shoulder to cry on, a reliable soul to confide in. However, more often than not, in our eagerness to seek individual success, we lose friends along the way and that has become part of the process.\nThe chaos and never-ending turmoil that accompanies everyday life is an aggressive obstacle in the pursuit of stable friendships. As students, we use this excuse to shrug off losing people who we once thought to be loved ones.\nInstead of viewing our lives as a race, where everyone is on their own path to some form of success, we must relearn to value the connections we make along the track. We must reevaluate the harm of radical, individual freedom.\nRadical individualism elevates the freedom of the individual over the collective. It argues, for instance, that universal healthcare, an example of collectivism, threatened the dream of many Americans.\nMany of us have fallen victim to an individualistic culture. Society tends to celebrate personal achievement and commend it based on the assumption that winning or succeeding is a consequence of working harder than the next person.\nAs a society, we idolize the concept of \"losing friends on the road to success\". I remember hearing in high school, \"Don't worry about losing friendships, just focus on study and getting into college. That's all that will matter in the long run,\" a sentiment that is not unique to my experience. It's preached in many success stories, and it's embedded in the way we see growth. Motivational speakers will push their audience to be complacent to the inevitable end of losing friends. Spend too much time with friends? You're losing time better utilized on your achievements. Invest time in your relationships? Invest time in your work instead.\nThis constant need to succeed has blinded us from seeing through the whirlwind of change that accompanies our everyday lives. We are expected to tangibly succeed, earn awards, get higher positions, earn better grades. In order to succeed, we lose friends, and that has sadly become normalized.\nWe cannot be complacent with losing friendships because they are our anchors. Yes, pursue success. Challenge yourself. Push limits. But contrary to all the self-proclaimed success experts, doing it alone does not make you more successful or any better than someone who achieved it with the help of others.\nPut effort into fostering healthy relationships, and maintaining them through your busy schedule that seems like it leaves no time for anyone else. Although we may be living in a society that treasures the independent pursuit of achievement, having people to support and love you on the way is far more important than any end result.", "50": "Many see friendships as a comfort blanket: a shoulder to cry on, a reliable soul to confide in. However, more often than not, in our eagerness to seek individual success, we lose friends along the way and that has become part of the process.\nThe chaos and never-ending turmoil that accompanies everyday life is an aggressive obstacle in the pursuit of stable friendships. As students, we use this excuse to shrug off losing people who we once thought to be loved ones.\nInstead of viewing our lives as a race, where everyone is on their own path to some form of success, we must relearn to value the connections we make along the track. We must reevaluate the harm of radical, individual freedom.\nRadical individualism elevates the freedom of the individual over the collective. It argues, for instance, that universal healthcare, an example of collectivism, threatened the dream of many Americans.\nMany of us have fallen victim to an individualistic culture. Society tends to celebrate personal achievement and commend it based on the assumption that winning or succeeding is a consequence of working harder than the next person.\nAs a society, we idolize the concept of \"losing friends on the road to success\". I remember hearing in high school, \"Don't worry about losing friendships, just focus on study and getting into college. That's all that will matter in the long run,\" a sentiment that is not unique to my experience. It's preached in many success stories, and it's embedded in the way we see growth. Motivational speakers will push their audience to be complacent to the inevitable end of losing friends. Spend too much time with friends? You're losing time better utilized on your achievements. Invest time in your relationships? Invest time in your work instead.\nThis constant need to succeed has blinded us from seeing through the whirlwind of change that accompanies our everyday lives. We are expected to tangibly succeed, earn awards, get higher positions, earn better grades. In order to succeed, we lose friends, and that has sadly become normalized.\nWe cannot be complacent with losing friendships because they are our anchors. Yes, pursue success. Challenge yourself. Push limits. But contrary to all the self-proclaimed success experts, doing it alone does not make you more successful or any better than someone who achieved it with the help of others.\nPut effort into fostering healthy relationships, and maintaining them through your busy schedule that seems like it leaves no time for anyone else. Although we may be living in a society that treasures the independent pursuit of achievement, having people to support and love you on the way is far more important than any end result.", "51": "During his acceptance speech for the Nobel Peace Prize, Martin Luther King Jr. said, \"I believe that unarmed truth and unconditional love will have the final word in reality.\"\nMore than five decades later, our society is still struggling to separate truth from fantasy, fact from fiction. From disputes over the climate change to debates about how slavery is taught, America is at a crossroads. And the battle for equity is at the heart of these vital conversations.\nStudy after study shows that inequity has had appalling consequences. A 2020 report by Citi indicated that \"Black students could have increased their lifetime incomes $90 – $113 billion\" if equity issues related to higher education had been adequately addressed over the past two decades.\nThis statistic represents a flood of issues we've failed to confront as a nation. It also highlights one truth: We're missing out on valuable opportunities to bridge the differences that divide us. However, by honoring King's legacy, we can get our country back on the path toward true equity.\nStudents — and adults for that matter — simply cannot understand the conflicts we have today without understanding what caused them in the first place. That means we have a responsibility to help them make connections between US history and current events. It also means giving them the tools they need to address inequity and bias in their own lives. Adherence to these principles, along with a commitment to truthful dialogue, can help every student better appreciate how far we've come and more clearly see the roads we have yet to travel.\nWith more truthful analysis of the need for more qualified, diverse educators, we can improve our recruiting, training, and retention strategies for schools across the country.\nUnfortunately, our deeply rooted divisions are delaying these advancements. A recent survey found that 77 percent of Americans believe the nation is \"more divided than ever before\". But reports of increased polarization shouldn't persuade us to look away from one another. Instead, they should compel us to address our shared issues head-on.\nWe are not, as some would suggest, living in a \"post-racial society\". We simply cannot afford to keep our heads buried in the sand when it comes to racial inequity. Unless we get honest about intolerance, bias, and how these social diseases impact the ways we treat one another, injustice will continue to haunt us. But our children and our grandchildren deserve better.\nOn this Martin Luther King Day and beyond, let's commit to working together to disrupt the status quo. Let's continue to build a better, more inclusive nation; and in the words of Martin Luther King Jr., \"A society at peace with itself, a society that can live with its conscience.\"", "52": "During his acceptance speech for the Nobel Peace Prize, Martin Luther King Jr. said, \"I believe that unarmed truth and unconditional love will have the final word in reality.\"\nMore than five decades later, our society is still struggling to separate truth from fantasy, fact from fiction. From disputes over the climate change to debates about how slavery is taught, America is at a crossroads. And the battle for equity is at the heart of these vital conversations.\nStudy after study shows that inequity has had appalling consequences. A 2020 report by Citi indicated that \"Black students could have increased their lifetime incomes $90 – $113 billion\" if equity issues related to higher education had been adequately addressed over the past two decades.\nThis statistic represents a flood of issues we've failed to confront as a nation. It also highlights one truth: We're missing out on valuable opportunities to bridge the differences that divide us. However, by honoring King's legacy, we can get our country back on the path toward true equity.\nStudents — and adults for that matter — simply cannot understand the conflicts we have today without understanding what caused them in the first place. That means we have a responsibility to help them make connections between US history and current events. It also means giving them the tools they need to address inequity and bias in their own lives. Adherence to these principles, along with a commitment to truthful dialogue, can help every student better appreciate how far we've come and more clearly see the roads we have yet to travel.\nWith more truthful analysis of the need for more qualified, diverse educators, we can improve our recruiting, training, and retention strategies for schools across the country.\nUnfortunately, our deeply rooted divisions are delaying these advancements. A recent survey found that 77 percent of Americans believe the nation is \"more divided than ever before\". But reports of increased polarization shouldn't persuade us to look away from one another. Instead, they should compel us to address our shared issues head-on.\nWe are not, as some would suggest, living in a \"post-racial society\". We simply cannot afford to keep our heads buried in the sand when it comes to racial inequity. Unless we get honest about intolerance, bias, and how these social diseases impact the ways we treat one another, injustice will continue to haunt us. But our children and our grandchildren deserve better.\nOn this Martin Luther King Day and beyond, let's commit to working together to disrupt the status quo. Let's continue to build a better, more inclusive nation; and in the words of Martin Luther King Jr., \"A society at peace with itself, a society that can live with its conscience.\"", "53": "During his acceptance speech for the Nobel Peace Prize, Martin Luther King Jr. said, \"I believe that unarmed truth and unconditional love will have the final word in reality.\"\nMore than five decades later, our society is still struggling to separate truth from fantasy, fact from fiction. From disputes over the climate change to debates about how slavery is taught, America is at a crossroads. And the battle for equity is at the heart of these vital conversations.\nStudy after study shows that inequity has had appalling consequences. A 2020 report by Citi indicated that \"Black students could have increased their lifetime incomes $90 – $113 billion\" if equity issues related to higher education had been adequately addressed over the past two decades.\nThis statistic represents a flood of issues we've failed to confront as a nation. It also highlights one truth: We're missing out on valuable opportunities to bridge the differences that divide us. However, by honoring King's legacy, we can get our country back on the path toward true equity.\nStudents — and adults for that matter — simply cannot understand the conflicts we have today without understanding what caused them in the first place. That means we have a responsibility to help them make connections between US history and current events. It also means giving them the tools they need to address inequity and bias in their own lives. Adherence to these principles, along with a commitment to truthful dialogue, can help every student better appreciate how far we've come and more clearly see the roads we have yet to travel.\nWith more truthful analysis of the need for more qualified, diverse educators, we can improve our recruiting, training, and retention strategies for schools across the country.\nUnfortunately, our deeply rooted divisions are delaying these advancements. A recent survey found that 77 percent of Americans believe the nation is \"more divided than ever before\". But reports of increased polarization shouldn't persuade us to look away from one another. Instead, they should compel us to address our shared issues head-on.\nWe are not, as some would suggest, living in a \"post-racial society\". We simply cannot afford to keep our heads buried in the sand when it comes to racial inequity. Unless we get honest about intolerance, bias, and how these social diseases impact the ways we treat one another, injustice will continue to haunt us. But our children and our grandchildren deserve better.\nOn this Martin Luther King Day and beyond, let's commit to working together to disrupt the status quo. Let's continue to build a better, more inclusive nation; and in the words of Martin Luther King Jr., \"A society at peace with itself, a society that can live with its conscience.\"", "54": "During his acceptance speech for the Nobel Peace Prize, Martin Luther King Jr. said, \"I believe that unarmed truth and unconditional love will have the final word in reality.\"\nMore than five decades later, our society is still struggling to separate truth from fantasy, fact from fiction. From disputes over the climate change to debates about how slavery is taught, America is at a crossroads. And the battle for equity is at the heart of these vital conversations.\nStudy after study shows that inequity has had appalling consequences. A 2020 report by Citi indicated that \"Black students could have increased their lifetime incomes $90 – $113 billion\" if equity issues related to higher education had been adequately addressed over the past two decades.\nThis statistic represents a flood of issues we've failed to confront as a nation. It also highlights one truth: We're missing out on valuable opportunities to bridge the differences that divide us. However, by honoring King's legacy, we can get our country back on the path toward true equity.\nStudents — and adults for that matter — simply cannot understand the conflicts we have today without understanding what caused them in the first place. That means we have a responsibility to help them make connections between US history and current events. It also means giving them the tools they need to address inequity and bias in their own lives. Adherence to these principles, along with a commitment to truthful dialogue, can help every student better appreciate how far we've come and more clearly see the roads we have yet to travel.\nWith more truthful analysis of the need for more qualified, diverse educators, we can improve our recruiting, training, and retention strategies for schools across the country.\nUnfortunately, our deeply rooted divisions are delaying these advancements. A recent survey found that 77 percent of Americans believe the nation is \"more divided than ever before\". But reports of increased polarization shouldn't persuade us to look away from one another. Instead, they should compel us to address our shared issues head-on.\nWe are not, as some would suggest, living in a \"post-racial society\". We simply cannot afford to keep our heads buried in the sand when it comes to racial inequity. Unless we get honest about intolerance, bias, and how these social diseases impact the ways we treat one another, injustice will continue to haunt us. But our children and our grandchildren deserve better.\nOn this Martin Luther King Day and beyond, let's commit to working together to disrupt the status quo. Let's continue to build a better, more inclusive nation; and in the words of Martin Luther King Jr., \"A society at peace with itself, a society that can live with its conscience.\"", "55": "During his acceptance speech for the Nobel Peace Prize, Martin Luther King Jr. said, \"I believe that unarmed truth and unconditional love will have the final word in reality.\"\nMore than five decades later, our society is still struggling to separate truth from fantasy, fact from fiction. From disputes over the climate change to debates about how slavery is taught, America is at a crossroads. And the battle for equity is at the heart of these vital conversations.\nStudy after study shows that inequity has had appalling consequences. A 2020 report by Citi indicated that \"Black students could have increased their lifetime incomes $90 – $113 billion\" if equity issues related to higher education had been adequately addressed over the past two decades.\nThis statistic represents a flood of issues we've failed to confront as a nation. It also highlights one truth: We're missing out on valuable opportunities to bridge the differences that divide us. However, by honoring King's legacy, we can get our country back on the path toward true equity.\nStudents — and adults for that matter — simply cannot understand the conflicts we have today without understanding what caused them in the first place. That means we have a responsibility to help them make connections between US history and current events. It also means giving them the tools they need to address inequity and bias in their own lives. Adherence to these principles, along with a commitment to truthful dialogue, can help every student better appreciate how far we've come and more clearly see the roads we have yet to travel.\nWith more truthful analysis of the need for more qualified, diverse educators, we can improve our recruiting, training, and retention strategies for schools across the country.\nUnfortunately, our deeply rooted divisions are delaying these advancements. A recent survey found that 77 percent of Americans believe the nation is \"more divided than ever before\". But reports of increased polarization shouldn't persuade us to look away from one another. Instead, they should compel us to address our shared issues head-on.\nWe are not, as some would suggest, living in a \"post-racial society\". We simply cannot afford to keep our heads buried in the sand when it comes to racial inequity. Unless we get honest about intolerance, bias, and how these social diseases impact the ways we treat one another, injustice will continue to haunt us. But our children and our grandchildren deserve better.\nOn this Martin Luther King Day and beyond, let's commit to working together to disrupt the status quo. Let's continue to build a better, more inclusive nation; and in the words of Martin Luther King Jr., \"A society at peace with itself, a society that can live with its conscience.\""}, "questions": [{"id": "2025-12-1-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "What do we learn about the man from the conversation?", "options": ["A) He has distinguished himself in foreign service.", "B) He is his country's ambassador to Winopia.", "C) He is currently an electrical engineer.", "D) He craves attention from the media."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） He has distinguished himself in foreign service.\n【定位】Today's special guest is Lord Blackwell, one of our country's most experienced and\ndistinguished career diplomats.\n今天的特邀嘉宾是布莱克韦尔勋爵，他是我国最有经验、最杰出的职业外交官之一。\n【信号】主持人开场介绍嘉宾的那一句。答案信号是\"访谈开场主持人对嘉宾的定性介绍\"——one of\nour country's most experienced and distinguished career diplomats，访谈类对话里嘉\n宾身份几乎都在开场这句一锤定音。\n【替换】distinguished career diplomats ↔ distinguished himself in foreign service\ndiplomats（外交官）= foreign service（外交事务）的同义改写\n【排除】B) 未提及\n录音里 Winopia 只是被问及的新闻话题，男方明确说自己 never been involved with\nWinopian relations（从未涉足对 Winopia 的事务），根本不是该国大使。选项\n借\"Winopia\"这个反复出现的地名诱你误选。\n借⽤原⽂：I've never been involved with Winopian relations\nC) 原词陷阱\n男方说自己 I got a degree in electrical engineering，那只是早年学历，他随后转空军、\n读法律、入外交界已 22 年。选项用 currently（目前）把旧学历说成现职，是典型\"听到\nelectrical engineer 就想选\"的原词陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：I got a degree in electrical engineering\nD) 未提及\n通篇是主持人邀他来谈外交，男方对热点新闻还谦称只知道新闻上看到的，没有任何\"爱出\n风头\"的迹象。He craves attention from the media. 在录音里毫无依据。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "What does the man say the government of Winopia is probably undergoing?", "options": ["A) A government reshuffle.", "B) A fundamental reform.", "C) Some sort of political reorientation.", "D) Some sort of crisis of legitimacy."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Some sort of crisis of legitimacy.\n【定位】It looks like the government of Winopia seems to be undergoing some sort of crisis of\nlegitimacy, and an interim administration has been transitionally established until a head of\nstate is agreed on.\n看起来 Winopia 政府似乎正在经历某种合法性危机，已经过渡性地成立了临时政府，直到\n选定国家元首为止。\n【信号】被问方对局势直接下定性结论的应答。答案信号是\"被问者的直接应答\"：主持人问 Winopia\n现状，男方应答里直接给出定性 undergoing some sort of crisis of legitimacy，对话题答\n案就落在这种\"针对提问的正面回答\"里。\n【替换】some sort of crisis of legitimacy ↔ Some sort of crisis of legitimacy\n答案句与选项几乎原句对应\n【排除】A) 未提及\nA government reshuffle 指内阁人事调整，远轻于录音所述的 crisis of legitimacy。录音\n根本没提改组，是用一个程度更轻的政治术语来稀释正解。\nB) 未提及\nA fundamental reform\"根本性改革\"带积极色彩，而录音说的是危机加临时政府的被动局\n面，色彩相反且录音从未提\"改革\"。\nC) 原词陷阱\nSome sort of political reorientation 故意复制录音的 some sort of 句式来制造耳熟，但\n录音里 some sort of 后接的是 crisis of legitimacy，不是 political reorientation。盗的是\n句式不是内容。\n借⽤原⽂：undergoing some sort of crisis of legitimacy", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "According to the man, how does one get a career in foreign relations?", "options": ["A) All diplomats have more or less similar stories to tell.", "B) It is a prerequisite to get some practice in diplomacy.", "C) Different people can take different paths.", "D) It is a must to first obtain a law degree."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Different people can take different paths.\n【定位】There are many different paths one can take. All diplomats have their own story.\n可以走的路有很多条。每个外交官都有自己的故事。\n【信号】应答开头的总括句，先于个人举例。答案信号是\"被问方应答的第一句总括\"——There are\nmany different paths one can take。它出现在男方随后讲自己经历（电气—空军—法\n律）之前，总括句先于例子，正是答案所在；后面的个人路径只是举例佐证。\n【替换】There are many different paths one can take ↔ Different people can take different paths\nmany different paths = different people take different paths，多样性同义\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\n录音 All diplomats have their own story 强调\"各有各的\"，选项 All diplomats have\nmore or less similar stories to tell 借 All diplomats…story 的外壳，把 own（各自的）\n偷换成 similar（相似的），意思正好反了。\n借⽤原⽂：All diplomats have their own story\nB) 与原文相反\nIt is a prerequisite to get some practice in diplomacy. 把\"实习\"说成 prerequisite（必\n要前提），但男方主张路径多样、从未说存在任何\"必备前提\"，绝对化表述与\"因人而异\"相\n悖。\nD) 原词陷阱\n男方确实提到自己 obtaining a degree in international law，那只是他个人路径的一环。\n选项 It is a must to first obtain a law degree. 把个人经历拔高成对所有人的\"必须\"，是听\n到 law degree 就想选的原词陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：upon obtaining a degree in international law", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "What do the speakers mainly discuss by the end of the conversation?", "options": ["A) Key skills required of a diplomat.", "B) The making of an expert diplomat.", "C) Diplomatic discourse and rhetoric.", "D) Contributions by diplomatic envoys."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （主旨题） Key skills required of a diplomat.\n【定位】W: What would you say are the key skills one needs for the job? M: There are so many. Being\na hard worker and a good student is important... a diplomat needs to be very articulate in their\ndiscourse and rhetoric...people and communication skills are vital.\n女：你认为这份工作需要哪些关键技能？男：太多了。做个勤奋的人、好学的人很重要……\n外交官在言辞和修辞上要非常善于表达……为人处世和沟通能力至关重要。\n【信号】结尾一问一答围着\"关键技能\"展开。答案信号是\"结尾这轮问答的共同焦点\"：主持人问 the\nkey skills one needs for the job，男方随后整段都在列举各种 skills，问与答合起来锁定\n主话题。主旨题靠这种\"问答合判\"，不靠单句。\n【替换】the key skills one needs for the job ↔ Key skills required of a diplomat\nkey skills…for the job = Key skills required of a diplomat，几乎逐词对应\n【排除】B) 过度推断\nThe making of an expert diplomat 谈的是\"怎样成长为专家\"的全过程，而结尾只在列举\n工作所需技能，没讲成长路径。它把话题撑得比实际更大。\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音确说 articulate in their discourse and rhetoric，但那只是男方列举的众多技能中的\n一项，Diplomatic discourse and rhetoric 拿一个细节当整段主题，以偏概全。\n借⽤原⽂：very articulate in their discourse and rhetoric\nD) 未提及\n结尾谈的是技能，没有讨论外交官做出了什么贡献。Contributions by diplomatic envoys.\n在结尾内容里没有依据（envoys 一词虽前文出现过，但话题不是贡献）。\n借⽤原⽂：our expert envoys at our consulate", "points": ["听力·长对话", "主旨题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "Why does the man suggest they call it a day?", "options": ["A) They have got their work done.", "B) They both look tired", "C) They can meet again tomorrow anyway.", "D) They are both anxious to go home."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） They both look tired\n【定位】It's getting late and we're both displaying signs of weariness. I think we should call it a day\nand meet again tomorrow morning.\n已经很晚了，我俩都显出疲态了。我觉得今天就到这儿吧，明天上午再继续。\n【信号】提议收工那句里紧贴的理由状语。男方说 I think we should call it a day（提议收工）之前\n紧跟着 we're both displaying signs of weariness（我俩都显出疲态），这个与提议直接\n相连的状态描述就是收工理由，是答案信号。\n【替换】we're both displaying signs of weariness ↔ They both look tired\nweariness 改写成 tired，displaying signs 改写成 look\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nThey have got their work done. 借了原文反复出现的 done（如 we are almost done、\nwe will be all done）来诱选，但那些 done 都带 almost / will be，是『快完了 / 将会完\n成』，并非已经干完；男方收工理由是累，不是完工。\n借⽤原⽂：Don't you think we are almost done?\nC) 答非所问\nThey can meet again tomorrow anyway. 取自男方 meet again tomorrow morning，\n但那是收工之后的安排，不是他『为什么要收工』的理由，与 Why 的问法错位。\n借⽤原⽂：meet again tomorrow morning\nD) 未提及\nThey are both anxious to go home. 里 anxious to go home（急切回家）原文没有对应\n表述；男方只说 call it a day（今天到此），是因为累，并未表现出急切回家的情绪。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "What does the woman say is a little tough?", "options": ["A) The board members' insufficient support.", "B) Two months devoted to the same contract.", "C) Working two months without any progress.", "D) The man's long concentration on the project."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Two months devoted to the same contract.\n【定位】Two months working on nothing but the same contract is a little tough.\n整整两个月只扑在同一份合同上，是有点难熬。\n【信号】题干同词 tough 在原文的复现处即落点。女方原话 Two months working on nothing\nbut the same contract is a little tough 里出现了与题干完全一样的 a little tough，这个\n评价词所修饰的主语就是答案句的核心。\n【替换】Two months working on nothing but the same contract ↔\nTwo months devoted to the same contract.\nworking on nothing but 改写成 devoted to\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\nThe board members' insufficient support. 里 board members 确在录音出现，但谈董事\n会的是男方在 P13 段的牢骚，且女方在 the board, too, is virtually unanimous in its\nsupport 反而说董事会几乎一致支持；把这话安到女方说 tough 头上，是张冠李戴。\n借⽤原⽂：demands rectifications be made\nC) 与原文相反\nWorking two months without any progress. 多出的 without any progress 与原文相\n反：女方明说 I think we are making good progress（进展不错），可见难的是『两个月\n只干一件事』的枯燥，而非没进展。B、C 一字之差，差就差在这里。\n借⽤原⽂：I think we are making good progress\nD) 张冠李戴\nThe man's long concentration on the project. 说的是男方对项目投入太久（呼应 it's\njust been so long），但题目问的是女方说哪件事 tough；把男方的体验安给女方所评价\n的难事，主体错位。\n借⽤原⽂：it's just been so long", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "What does the man say the woman must be in thinking they'll finish by next week?", "options": ["A) Unprofessional.", "B) Inspirational.", "C) Exceptional.", "D) Delusional."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （态度观点题） Delusional.\n【定位】Ha, you must be delusional.\n哈，你一定是在痴心妄想吧。\n【信号】男方对女方乐观预期的直接回怼。女方在 we'll be done by next week 表达乐观，男方紧\n接着 you must be delusional 直接回应——这句被问者 / 回应方给出的评价就是答案信\n号，语气还带 Ha 的讥讽。\n【替换】you must be delusional ↔ Delusional.\n原句评价词直接成选项\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\nUnprofessional. 与 delusional 同为贬义，易混；但 delusional 指『脱离现实、妄想』，\n针对的是女方对工期的乐观判断，并非批评她职业水准。色彩对了，落点错了。\nB) 与原文相反\nInspirational.（鼓舞人心）是褒义，与男方 Ha, you must be delusional 的讥讽否定语气\n完全相反，听出语气是贬即可排除。\n借⽤原⽂：Ha, you must be delusional\nC) 与原文相反\nExceptional.（出色的）同为褒义，男方并未夸女方，而是讥讽她想法不现实，褒贬方向就\n不对。\n借⽤原⽂：you must be delusional", "points": ["听力·长对话", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "What does the entire managerial hierarchy want to see according to the woman?", "options": ["A) Fewer rectifications of the contract.", "B) More support from the board members.", "C) The early implementation of the project.", "D) The alignment of all sections of the contract."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） The early implementation of the project.\n【定位】the entire managerial hierarchy wants to see this project implemented as soon as possible\n整个管理层都希望这个项目尽快落地实施。\n【信号】愿望词 wants to see 直接领出答案。女方 the entire managerial hierarchy wants to\nsee this project implemented as soon as possible 里 wants to see 与题干 want to see\n完全对应，其后内容即所求愿望，是明确的答案信号。\n【替换】this project implemented as soon as possible ↔ The early implementation of the project.\n动词 implemented + as soon as possible 改写成名词 early implementation\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nFewer rectifications of the contract. 借了男方 demands rectifications be made 里的\nrectifications，但那是男方预测董事会会要求修改的牢骚，不是管理层『想看到』的，且主\n体是男方说的不是女方说的管理层愿望。\n借⽤原⽂：demands rectifications be made\nB) 偷换概念\nMore support from the board members. 盗用 the board, too, is virtually unanimous\nin its support 里的 support，但女方说的是董事会已经几乎一致支持，并非管理层『想要\n更多支持』，把现状偷换成了诉求。\n借⽤原⽂：the board, too, is virtually unanimous in its support\nD) 张冠李戴\nThe alignment of all sections of the contract. 盗用男方 Sections 4, 5, 8 and 9 still\nhave to be aligned to the new framework 里的 aligned / sections，那是男方列举的剩\n余工作，不是女方所说管理层想看到的结果。\n借⽤原⽂：Sections 4, 5, 8 and 9 still have to be aligned to the new framework", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "When can it be hard to know whom to believe?", "options": ["A) When two employees propose contrary solutions to their dispute.", "B) When two employees are directed by their respective manager.", "C) When two people behave in an apparently clashing manner.", "D) When two people tell different stories of the same event."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） When two people tell different stories of the same event.\n【定位】When two people have different versions of the same event, it can be hard to know whom\nto believe.\n当两个人对同一件事有不同的版本时，就很难知道该相信谁。\n【信号】题干原句几乎照搬。原文 When two people have different versions of the same event,\nit can be hard to know whom to believe. 与题干用了同一个结尾 hard to know whom\nto believe，这种题干与原文句尾高度吻合的呼应，就是答案句的定位信号——前半的\nWhen 从句即是要找的情形。\n【替换】different versions of the same event ↔ different stories of the same event\nversions 改写成 stories\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 用了原文出现的 dispute 一词，但把情形偷换成 propose contrary solutions to their\ndispute（提出相反的解决方案），而原文说的是对同一事件有不同版本，根本没提双方提\n解决方案，属听到原词就想选的陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：there may not always be clear right-or-wrong solutions to employee disputes\nB) 未提及\nB 说情形是 two employees are directed by their respective manager（两员工被各自经\n理指派），原文只提到管理者后来介入处理，从未说难辨真假是因为被经理指派，是未提及\n的捏造。\nC) 偷换概念\nC 把情形说成 behave in an apparently clashing manner（行为上看起来冲突），但原文\n界定的是对同一事件说法不同，是认知/陈述层面的分歧，不是行为冲突，偷换了情形的性\n质。\n借⽤原⽂：different versions of the same event", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "What does the speaker definitely recall about the dispute mentioned as an example?", "options": ["A) How it got resolved.", "B) How it happened.", "C) What it was about.", "D) When it was settled."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） How it got resolved.\n【定位】It happened years ago, and it's a measure of the forgettable nature of the conflict that I no\nlonger even recall what the dispute was about, but I definitely recall the resolution.\n那是多年前的事了，这场冲突有多无足轻重可见一斑——我甚至已不记得争执的内容是什\n么，但我清楚地记得它是怎么解决的。\n【信号】but 转折后才是落点。句中 I no longer even recall what the dispute was about, but I\ndefinitely recall the resolution 用 but 把前半的不记得内容和后半的记得解决方式分隔\n开，转折后的 definitely recall the resolution 才是说话人真正强调、也是题干 definitely\nrecall 所问的落点。\n【替换】I definitely recall the resolution ↔ How it got resolved\n名词 resolution 化为问句方式\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nB 用了原文出现的 happened（It happened years ago），但那只是交代时间久远，说话\n人记得的是解决方式不是经过；听到 happened 就选 B 正中圈套。\n借⽤原⽂：It happened years ago\nC) 与原文相反\nC 问 What it was about（关于什么），但原文恰恰说 I no longer even recall what the\ndispute was about——这正是说话人不记得的那一面，与题干 definitely recall 正好相\n反。\n借⽤原⽂：I no longer even recall what the dispute was about\nD) 偷换概念\nD 问 When it was settled（何时解决），与 A 都涉及解决，但原文记得的是 the\nresolution（怎么解决）而非解决的时间点，原文只用 years ago 泛指年代，并未交代具体\n何时 settled，属把方式偷换成时间。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "What did the speaker and his counterpart do when the two employees gave different accounts of the same event?", "options": ["A) They intervened promptly lest their dispute escalate.", "B) They tried to ascertain the actual facts of the matter.", "C) They assumed the best about them and moved on.", "D) They persuaded them to put their conflict aside."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） They assumed the best about them and moved on.\n【定位】We agreed to monitor their actions and relationship going forward, but to intervene no further.\nWe assumed the best about both of them and moved on.\n我们商定今后留意他们的言行和关系，但不再进一步介入。我们对两人都往好处想，然后向\n前看了。\n【信号】结论句直陈所做。原文 We assumed the best about both of them and moved on 是对\n他们行动的直接陈述句，紧接在 to intervene no further 之后收束整段处理过程，是叙述\n实际所做的结论落点。\n【替换】We assumed the best about both of them and moved on ↔\nThey assumed the best about them and moved on\n几乎原句保留只换主语\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nA 说他们 intervened promptly lest their dispute escalate（及时介入防升级），但原文\n明说 to intervene no further——决定不再介入，A 的方向与原文正好相反。\n借⽤原⽂：to intervene no further\nB) 原词陷阱\nB 用了原文原话 ascertain the actual facts of the matter，但原文是 we might never\nascertain the actual facts of the matter——他们认定查不出真相、放弃了查，B 把这句\n截头去尾说成他们努力查清，恰好弄反。\n借⽤原⽂：we might never ascertain the actual facts of the matter\nD) 未提及\nD 说他们 persuaded them to put their conflict aside（劝员工搁置冲突），但原文是两\n位管理者自己决定 moved on，并未去劝说员工放下，是未提及的动作；原文只说 put the\nconflict behind us，主语是管理者而非劝员工。\n借⽤原⽂：we were able to put the conflict behind us", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "What do an increasing number of Americans do according to the passage?", "options": ["A) Dine out daily.", "B) Budget sensibly.", "C) Enjoy cooking.", "D) Shop at groceries."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Dine out daily.\n【定位】For others, it's just a daily occurrence. And that second group of people is becoming the\nmajority as the number of Americans who enjoy cooking is declining.\n对另一些人来说，外出吃饭只是每天都在发生的平常事。而随着喜欢做饭的美国人数量在下\n降，这第二类人正在成为多数。\n【信号】becoming the majority锁定增多的群体。答案信号是 becoming the majority（正在成\n为多数），它紧接 that second group of people；而\"第二类人\"指的正是前一句的 it's\njust a daily occurrence，即每天外出就餐的人。\"成为多数\"就是\"越来越多\"的信号落点。\n【替换】it's just a daily occurrence ↔ Dine out daily.\n外出就餐+每天的同义改写\nthat second group of people is becoming the majority ↔ Dine out daily.\n成为多数对应\"越来越多人做\"\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n原文开头说第一类人把外食当作 planned and budgeted for 的款待，选项 Budget\nsensibly. 盗用了 budget 一词，但\"做预算\"是第一类（少数人）的做法，不是题干问的\"越\n来越多的人\"，属原词陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：planned and budgeted for\nC) 与原文相反\n选项 Enjoy cooking. 抓住了原文的 cooking 和 enjoy，但原文是 the number of\nAmericans who enjoy cooking is declining——喜欢做饭的人在\"减少\"，正与题\n干\"increasing\"相反，方向反了。\n借⽤原⽂：the number of Americans who enjoy cooking is declining\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项 Shop at groceries. 的 grocery 一词原文后段确实出现于 challenges for the grocery\nindustry，但那是讲对杂货业的挑战，不是说\"越来越多人去杂货店买菜\"，原文从未把\"逛杂\n货店\"列为增多的行为，属原词陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：challenges for the grocery industry", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "What does Eddie Yoon think cooking is akin to?", "options": ["A) Shopping.", "B) Sewing.", "C) Entertaining oneself.", "D) Fashion designing."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Sewing.\n【定位】Yoon asserts cooking is akin to sewing. As recently as the early 20th century, many\nAmericans sewed their own clothing.\n尤恩断言，做饭好比缝纫。就在不久前的20世纪初，很多美国人还自己缝制衣服。\n【信号】akin to后面紧接的名词。答案信号就是类比词 akin to，它在原文里直接说出 cooking is\nakin to sewing，紧跟其后的 sewing 即被类比的对象，定位干净利落。\n【替换】cooking is akin to sewing ↔ Sewing.\n原词直接对应\n【排除】A) 未提及\n选项 Shopping. 与原文谈的\"买衣服\"概念沾边（buy clothing），但原文把做饭类比的\n是\"缝纫\"这一动作，从未说做饭类似\"购物\"，属未提及的张冠李戴。\nC) 张冠李戴\n选项 Entertaining oneself.（自娱自乐）与原文 they do it mainly as a hobby 里的\nhobby 略有联想，但 hobby 是说\"今天少数人把缝纫当爱好\"，并非把做饭类比成\"娱乐\"，\n属张冠李戴。\n借⽤原⽂：they do it mainly as a hobby\nD) 偷换概念\n选项 Fashion designing.（服装设计）与原文反复出现的 clothing / sewed their own\nclothing 同属\"衣服\"话题，最易因联想而误选，但原文类比的是\"缝纫\"这个动作本身，不\n是\"服装设计\"，是话题相近的偷换。\n借⽤原⽂：many Americans sewed their own clothing", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "What will Americans be able to do by making their own lunch?", "options": ["A) Satisfy their taste.", "B) Cut down expenses.", "C) Eat more healthily.", "D) Cultivate new hobbies."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Cut down expenses.\n【定位】People spend, on average, $10 per meal when they buy lunch. Making your own lunch,\nmeanwhile, will cost less. One survey found people only spend on average $6.30 when they\nbring lunch prepared at home.\n人们买午餐平均每顿花10美元。而自己做午餐则会更便宜。一项调查发现，自带在家做好的\n午餐时，人们平均只花6.30美元。\n【信号】cost less加$10对$6.30的数字对比。答案信号有两层：一是明示的 Making your own\nlunch, meanwhile, will cost less（更便宜），二是 \\$10 per meal 与 \\$6.30 的数字对\n比，二者都直指\"省钱\"，是篇章里典型的\"对比+数字\"答案信号。\n【替换】Making your own lunch, meanwhile, will cost less ↔ Cut down expenses.\ncost less与cut down expenses同义\nthey bring lunch prepared at home ↔ Cut down expenses.\n数字证实自己做更省\n【排除】A) 未提及\n选项 Satisfy their taste.（满足口味）听上去是自己做饭的常见好处，但原文这段只谈钱\n（cost less、\\$6.30），完全没提味道好不好，属未提及。\nC) 未提及\n选项 Eat more healthily.（吃得更健康）是\"自己做饭\"最容易让人联想到的好处，迷惑性\n强，但原文论证的全是花钱多少，没有任何关于\"健康/营养\"的表述，属未提及。\nD) 张冠李戴\n选项 Cultivate new hobbies.（培养新爱好）里的 hobby 概念来自前文讲缝纫的 they do\nit mainly as a hobby，与\"自己做午餐\"这一段无关，是把别处的 hobby 嫁接过来，属张冠\n李戴。\n借⽤原⽂：they do it mainly as a hobby", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "What does the passage say signifies a host of challenges for America's grocery industry?", "options": ["A) People's intense focus on convenience.", "B) People's deteriorating family finances.", "C) People's strong aversion to junk foods.", "D) People's increasing dislike of cooking."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） People's increasing dislike of cooking.\n【定位】Americans' growing aversion to spending time in the kitchen signifies a host of challenges for\nthe grocery industry.\n美国人越来越不愿在厨房里花时间，这预示着杂货业将面临一系列挑战。\n【信号】signifies连接现象与挑战。答案信号是因果动词 signifies a host of challenges，它把后\n果\"诸多挑战\"与紧挨在它前面的现象 Americans\\' growing aversion to spending time in\nthe kitchen 直接绑在一起，前因后果一句话讲完。\n【替换】Americans\\' growing aversion to spending time in the kitchen ↔\nPeople's increasing dislike of cooking.\n厌恶下厨=越来越不爱做饭的同义改写\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 People's intense focus on convenience. 借用了原文 what people save in\nconvenience 的 convenience，但原文 convenience 是讲\"省下的便利\"，并非\"对便利的\n强烈追求带来挑战\"，signifies 前挂的现象不是它，属原词陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：what people save in convenience, they lose in cash\nB) 张冠李戴\n选项 People's deteriorating family finances. 里的 family finances 来自原文最后一句\ndetrimentally impact family finances，但那是说\"不爱下厨\"也会\"损害家庭财务\"——家庭\n财务是被影响的后果之一，不是给杂货业带来挑战的原因，张冠李戴。\n借⽤原⽂：detrimentally impact family finances\nC) 原词陷阱\n选项 People's strong aversion to junk foods. 盗用了原文 growing aversion 的 aversion\n一词，但原文 aversion 的对象是\"在厨房花时间\"，不是\"垃圾食品\"，原文通篇未提 junk\nfood，属原词陷阱叠加无中生有。\n借⽤原⽂：growing aversion to spending time in the kitchen", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "What is the number-one reason a bag ends up lost according to this talk?", "options": ["A) Its owner's flight is delayed because of bad weather.", "B) Airlines cannot find its owner without an ID tag on it.", "C) Airline staff have not correctly coded its destination tag.", "D) Its owner has forgot to pick it up from the baggage claim."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Airlines cannot find its owner without an ID tag on it.\n【定位】But the number-one reason a bag ends up lost is that it has no ID tag, and airlines don't know\nto whom it belongs.\n但行李丢失的头号原因是它没有身份标签，于是航空公司不知道它属于谁。\n【信号】转折词 But 加最高级 number-one 双重锁定。前文用 Bad weather and flight delays,\nmiscoded destination tags and some people simply forgetting to pick up 一口气列了\n一堆原因(都是干扰)，紧接着用 But the number-one reason 转折并加最高级，这句才是\n真正要答的\"首要原因\"。转折 But + 最高级 number-one 是双重答案信号。\n【替换】it has no ID tag ↔ without an ID tag on it\nhas no = without 否定同义改写\nairlines don't know to whom it belongs ↔ Airlines cannot find its owner\n不知属于谁=找不到主人\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 项的 flight is delayed because of bad weather 直接盗用了录音 Bad weather and\nflight delays 的原词，但这只是 But 之前罗列的众多次要原因之一，不是 number-one，\n听到 bad weather 就选正中陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：Bad weather and flight delays\nC) 原词陷阱\nC 项 correctly coded its destination tag 对应录音里的 miscoded destination tags，原\n词照搬，但它同样属于 But 之前的次要原因罗列，并非 number-one，是和 A 同类的原词\n陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：miscoded destination tags\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 项 forgot to pick it up from the baggage claim 来自录音 some people simply\nforgetting to pick up one of their bags，又是罗列区原词，属次要原因，不符合\nnumber-one，依旧是原词陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：some people simply forgetting to pick up one of their bags", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "What may happen to your baggage even when there are sophisticated automated systems?", "options": ["A) Its tag may get torn off on the conveyor belt.", "B) Its tag may get stuck in the sorting machine.", "C) It may be sent to a wrong destination.", "D) It may be picked up by someone else."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Its tag may get torn off on the conveyor belt.\n【定位】Even so, all that technology may not save your baggage tag from being torn off on the\nconveyor belt, making you the owner of a bag with no name.\n即便如此，所有这些技术也未必能让你的行李标签免于在传送带上被扯掉，结果你成了一个\n没有名字的行李的主人。\n【信号】让步转折 Even so 引出真正后果。前文 Some airports have sophisticated automated\nsystems 大谈各种先进系统(对应题干 sophisticated automated systems)，随后 Even so\n让步转折，点出\"技术也救不了标签被扯掉\"——Even so 之后才是答案。\n【替换】your baggage tag from being torn off on the conveyor belt ↔\nIts tag may get torn off on the conveyor belt\n几乎原句对应稍作改写\nmay not save your baggage tag ↔ Its tag may get torn off\n未能保住=可能被扯掉\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nB 项 stuck in the sorting machine 盗用了录音 sorting machines that route baggage\nto the appropriate gate 里的 sorting machine 一词，但录音说分拣机是\"把行李送到正确\n登机口\"，根本没说标签会卡在里面，属答非所问的原词陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：sorting machines that route baggage to the appropriate gate\nC) 张冠李戴\nC 项 sent to a wrong destination 让人联想到第一段 miscoded destination tags，但那\n是讲\"丢失原因\"、且不在 Even so 转折后；本题问的是即便技术先进仍会发生的事，录音对\n应的是标签被扯掉，不是送错地，属张冠李戴。\n借⽤原⽂：miscoded destination tags\nD) 未提及\nD 项 picked up by someone else 在第二段 Even so 这句里根本没出现，录音说的是标签\n被扯掉、行李变得无名，没说被别人取走，属未提及。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "What does the speaker advise us to do at the end of the talk?", "options": ["A) Purchase irretrievably lost things at auction.", "B) Contact the airport staff to find our missing bag.", "C) Get the airline agent's phone number before boarding.", "D) Avoid packing valuable items in our checked baggage."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Avoid packing valuable items in our checked baggage.\n【定位】So never pack anything of real value in your checked baggage.\n所以，千万不要把任何真正贵重的东西放进你的托运行李里。\n【信号】结尾祈使句 never 直发忠告。末段以 So never pack anything of real value in your\nchecked baggage 起头，So 表收束、never 领出强忠告，正是题干 at the end 的建议落\n点；这种结尾祈使句就是答案信号。\n【替换】never pack anything of real value ↔ Avoid packing valuable items\nnever=Avoid 否定建议同义改写\nanything of real value ↔ valuable items\nof real value = valuable 名形互换\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 项 Purchase irretrievably lost things at auction 盗用了第四段 sold at auction 与\nirretrievably lost 的原词，但那是在讲无主行李最终的处置去向，不是讲者给\"我们\"的忠\n告，属答非所问的原词陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：sold at auction\nB) 与原文相反\nB 项 Contact the airport staff to find our missing bag 与结尾 so an agent can contact\nyou 方向相反：录音说\"留下电话以便客服能联系你\"，B 却说\"我们去联系工作人员找包\"，\n主被动颠倒，属与原文相反/张冠李戴。\n借⽤原⽂：so an agent can contact you\nC) 偷换概念\nC 项 Get the airline agent's phone number before boarding 盗用了结尾 along with\nyour phone number 的 phone number，但录音说的是\"把你自己的电话写在行李上\"，不\n是\"登机前去拿客服电话\"，偷换了 phone number 的归属与做法，属偷换概念的原词陷\n阱。\n借⽤原⽂：along with your phone number", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "What does the speaker say about using one's smartphone during a face-to- face conversation?", "options": ["A) It can be behaviorally hazardous.", "B) It can be especially disappointing.", "C) It can be socially harmful.", "D) It can be really embarrassing."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） It can be socially harmful.\n【定位】There's also evidence that this behavior is socially harmful.\n也有证据表明，这种行为对社交是有害的。\n【信号】提示证据的 evidence that 之后即结论。There's also evidence that this behavior is\nsocially harmful. 用 evidence that 引出对该行为的正式定性，这类'有证据表明…'结构后\n面紧跟的就是说话人要立的判断，是答案句信号。\n【替换】this behavior is socially harmful ↔ It can be socially harmful.\n几乎原句，只把 behavior 换成 It\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\nIt can be behaviorally hazardous. 借录音反复出现的 behavior 一词（如 this behavior）\n造误导，但录音说的是'对社交有害'，并未说该行为本身'危险'，属偷换概念。\n借⽤原⽂：this behavior is socially harmful\nB) 答非所问\nIt can be especially disappointing. 像是从 less satisfied with their face-to-face\ninteractions 联想出的'失望'，但录音把它定性为 socially harmful，'让人不太满意'只是表\n现之一，不是本题要的总定性。\n借⽤原⽂：less satisfied with their face-to-face interactions\nD) 未提及\nIt can be really embarrassing. 的'尴尬'在录音里完全没有出现，是凭'玩手机让人尴尬'的生\n活常识硬补的，属未提及。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "How did the participants in the new study respond regarding phone use during social interactions?", "options": ["A) They considered it inevitable.", "B) They held a negative view towards it.", "C) They were concerned with its effects.", "D) They saw it as a sign of social evolution."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （态度观点题） They held a negative view towards it.\n【定位】The participants had a negative view of phone use during social interactions.\n参与者对社交互动中使用手机持负面看法。\n【信号】主体 participants + 态度词 negative view。The participants had a negative view of\nphone use during social interactions. 在题干主体 participants 出场处直接给出态度词\nnegative view，主体与评价词同现，是态度题的答案句信号。\n【替换】The participants had a negative view ↔ They held a negative view towards it\n近乎原句，had→held、of→towards、加代词 They/it\n【排除】A) 未提及\nThey considered it inevitable. 说参与者认为玩手机'不可避免'，录音只说他们持负面看\n法，从未谈'是否不可避免'，属未提及。\nC) 原词陷阱\nThey were concerned with its effects. 借录音里反复出现的 effects（如 adverse\neffects）造干扰，但那是研究者发现的客观影响，本题问的是参与者的态度，参与者是'持\n负面看法'而非'担忧其影响'。\n借⽤原⽂：adverse effects on participants' sense of belonging\nD) 与原文相反\nThey saw it as a sign of social evolution. 把玩手机说成'社会进化的标志'（正面），与录\n音 negative view 极性完全相反，属与原文相反。\n借⽤原⽂：The participants had a negative view", "points": ["听力·讲座", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "What does the speaker say can result from the common behavior of using phones during face-to-face conversations?", "options": ["A) Elaborate forms of social contact.", "B) Ill effects on one's sense of pride.", "C) Poor relationships with others.", "D) Vicious cycles of detachment."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Vicious cycles of detachment.\n【定位】This common behavior can create vicious cycles of detachment even as we try to connect\nwith others.\n即便我们试图与他人建立联系，这种常见行为也会造成疏离的恶性循环。\n【信号】篇末 can create 引出最终后果。This common behavior can create vicious cycles of\ndetachment 出现在全篇最后一句，用 can create 直接引出该行为的最终后果，是篇末收\n束式答案句信号。\n【替换】vicious cycles of detachment ↔ Vicious cycles of detachment.\n原词短语整体复现，仅首字母大写\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nElaborate forms of social contact. 借录音 they elaborate our understanding 里的\nelaborate 造干扰，但那里 elaborate 是动词'使更丰富（我们的理解）'，与'精致的社交形\n式'毫无关系，且方向上录音讲的是坏后果，A 听着像好事。\n借⽤原⽂：they elaborate our understanding of a subtle but common aspect\nB) 答非所问\nIll effects on one's sense of pride. 借 self-esteem（自尊）改写成 pride，录音确实说过\n对自尊有不良影响，但那是中段提到的影响，本题问 result from 的最终后果（末句的\nvicious cycles），B 答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：adverse effects on participants' sense of belonging, need for meaning and self-esteem\nC) 答非所问\nPoor relationships with others. 像是从 the quality of the relationship 来的'人际变差'，\n意思虽与 D 相近，但本题答案信号句末句用的是 vicious cycles of detachment，C 既非该\n后果句的措辞、又是参与者对关系质量的评判而非最终后果，故不选。\n借⽤原⽂：the worse they considered both the quality of the interaction and the quality of\nthe relationship", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "What does the speaker say travelers commonly complain about?", "options": ["A) The ill treatment they get as tourists.", "B) The nonprofessional business they see.", "C) The impersonal accommodations.", "D) The many adventures on their way."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） The impersonal accommodations.\n【定位】Whatever the reason for traveling, a common complaint of travelers is the impersonal\naccommodations they find.\n无论出行的理由是什么，旅行者们一个共同的抱怨就是他们遇到的那种缺乏人情味的住宿。\n【信号】complaint 信号词直接引出。答案句里 a common complaint of travelers is 与题干\ncommonly complain about 的抱怨信号严丝合缝，complaint 之后紧跟的 the\nimpersonal accommodations 就是抱怨的对象，是典型的信号词后面接答案。\n【替换】a common complaint of travelers is the impersonal accommodations ↔\nThe impersonal accommodations.\n近乎原句压缩\n【排除】A) 未提及\nThe ill treatment they get as tourists. 把抱怨说成受差待遇，但原文从头到尾说的是住宿\n缺人情味、千篇一律，并没提任何被恶待、被歧视的内容，属于无中生有的升级。\nB) 原词陷阱\nThe nonprofessional business they see. 借用了原文 personal or professional business\n里的 professional 一词，但原文那句是说人们因私事或公务出行，根本没说他们抱怨不专\n业的生意，是听到 professional 就想选的原词陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：many venture forth on personal or professional business\nD) 与原文相反\nThe many adventures on their way. 说一路有许多奇遇，是正面体验，而题目问的是抱怨\n（负面）；原文也无奇遇之说，方向与抱怨正相反，自然不选。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "What is said about Bed and Breakfast arrangements?", "options": ["A) They have been the classic way to visit Europe.", "B) They attract tourists with location and decoration.", "C) They have long been competing with chain hotels.", "D) They are in fact a completely new idea in America."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） They have been the classic way to visit Europe.\n【定位】For decades, B&Bs have been the classic way to visit Europe, especially the British Isles.\nAnd in fact, they are not really a new idea in America.\n几十年来，B&B 一直是游览欧洲、尤其是不列颠群岛的经典方式。而且事实上，它们在美\n国也并非真正的新事物。\n【信号】For decades 强调由来已久。For decades, B&Bs have been the classic way to visit\nEurope 用 For decades（几十年来）加现在完成时强调长期如此，与下一句 not really a\nnew idea 一正一补都在说它由来已久、不是新事物，时间状语就是这里的答案信号。\n【替换】B&Bs have been the classic way to visit Europe ↔\nThey have been the classic way to visit Europe.\n几乎原句搬运\n【排除】B) 张冠李戴\nThey attract tourists with location and decoration. 说靠地段和装潢吸引游客，location\n一词确在原文第三段 not only the building itself, but the location 出现过，但那是讲房主\n开店要考虑的因素，不是说 B&B 整体靠地段装潢揽客，把后文的考量错安成对 B&B 的定\n性。\n借⽤原⽂：not only the building itself, but the location\nC) 未提及\nThey have long been competing with chain hotels. 提到与连锁酒店长期竞争，但原文\n只说 B&B 提供个性化、友好的住宿来对抗千篇一律的感觉，从未提到 chain hotels 或彼此\n竞争，属凭常识脑补出的内容。\nD) 与原文相反\nThey are in fact a completely new idea in America. 说在美国是全新事物，恰与原文\nthey are not really a new idea in America 相反——原文明确用 not 否定了新事物，选项\n却去掉否定取了反义，是漏听否定词导致的正话反说。\n借⽤原⽂：they are not really a new idea in America", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "What is the first thing you have to consider if you want to start a B&B business?", "options": ["A) Your financial status.", "B) Your family's consent.", "C) Your property.", "D) Your personality."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Your property.\n【定位】The first consideration is your property, not only the building itself, but the location.\n首先要考虑的是你的房产，不仅是建筑本身，还有它的地理位置。\n【信号】The first consideration 排序信号。The first consideration is your property 用 The\nfirst consideration 明示第一项考虑，与题干 the first thing to consider 完全对应，排序\n信号词直接锁定 property。\n【替换】The first consideration is your property ↔ Your property.\n原词直接对应\n【排除】A) 答非所问\nYour financial status. 财务状况听上去像开店头等大事，极易凭常识选中，但原文把财务排\n在 The final consideration is a financial one——是最后一项而非第一项，错在排序。\n借⽤原⽂：The final consideration is a financial one\nB) 偷换概念\nYour family's consent. 说要家人同意，原文只说要考虑 the lifestyle of you and your\nfamily——家人是否真心喜欢接待客人，谈的是生活方式契合度，并非要谁点头同意，是把\n原文意思偷换成了同意。\n借⽤原⽂：the lifestyle of you and your family\nD) 答非所问\nYour personality. 性格虽与原文 truly enjoy visitors（是否真心喜欢客人）沾边，但那属于\n第二项生活方式考量，且原文用的是 lifestyle 而非 personality；它既非第一项、也不是原\n文对首项的说法。\n借⽤原⽂：truly enjoy visitors", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "What does the speaker say potential B&B hosts must be realistic about?", "options": ["A) Costs for starting the business.", "B) The number of guests to receive.", "C) The facilities to provide.", "D) Opportunities for income."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Opportunities for income.\n【定位】The final consideration is a financial one, potential B&B hosts must be realistic about\nopportunities for income.\n最后一项考虑是财务方面的，准 B&B 房主必须对赚钱的机会抱有现实的预期。\n【信号】realistic about 原词搭桥。答案句 must be realistic about opportunities for income 与\n题干 must be realistic about 用词几乎一致，realistic about 后面紧跟的 opportunities\nfor income 就是要现实看待的对象，信号词直接搭桥。\n【替换】must be realistic about opportunities for income ↔ Opportunities for income.\n原句截取核心宾语\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nCosts for starting the business. 借了原文 low initial costs 里的 costs，但原文是说启动\n成本通常很低，并不是说要对成本抱现实预期；要现实看待的明明是收入，这里用真念过的\ncosts 设了反向陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：B&Bs usually have low initial costs\nB) 答非所问\nThe number of guests to receive. 说要现实看待接待客人的数量，原文确提到 at least\nfive rooms rented regularly（至少五间房常租出），但那是用来说明靠 B&B 谋生不易的\n例子，落点仍是收入机会，不是叫房主对客人数量本身抱现实预期。\n借⽤原⽂：at least five rooms rented regularly\nC) 张冠李戴\nThe facilities to provide. 说设施，facilities 一词出现在原文第三段讲房产的部分 facilities\nfor the traveler，那是第一项房产的考量，与本题问的财务上要现实看待什么不在一处，被\n跨段挪来当答案。\n借⽤原⽂：facilities for the traveler", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adolescents", "B) arbitrarily", "C) chronic", "D) cognitive", "E) concede", "F) disregard", "G) drop", "H) ever", "I) maneuver", "J) mortality", "K) particularly", "L) patterns", "M) refute", "N) stereotype", "O) vocal"], "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H) ever\n副词\n【词性槽】too…to 框架里嵌一个副词修饰 too\n【依据】ever 与 too…to 在疑问句中构成 \"是否真的太……以至于不能……\" 的强调\nBut is it __(26)__ too late to start? 是承接 \"运动有诸多益处\" 后的设问：是否\"真的\"太晚\n了？ever 在疑问句中作副词，与 too late 连用强调\"究竟、真的\"，自然过渡到第二段用研\n究数据回应这个疑问。\n【竞争词】B) arbitrarily 义不符——\"任意地太晚\"逻辑不通\narbitrarily 指\"任意地、武断地\"，描述的是行为的随意性，而too late to start问的是时间晚不\n晚，与\"任意\"不搭。\nK) particularly particularly 留给第 33 题更贴\nparticularly 意为\"尤其\"，虽可修饰 too late 但语气勉强；且第 33 题 The latter is __(33)__\nimportant given the global obesity epidemic. 中\"尤其重要\"是 particularly 的经典搭配，留\n给那里更合身。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·too…to 框架里嵌一个副词修饰 to"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adolescents", "B) arbitrarily", "C) chronic", "D) cognitive", "E) concede", "F) disregard", "G) drop", "H) ever", "I) maneuver", "J) mortality", "K) particularly", "L) patterns", "M) refute", "N) stereotype", "O) vocal"], "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J) mortality\n名词\n【词性槽】形容词 early 后接名词作 of 短语的宾语\n【依据】risk of early mortality = 早死风险，是流行病学固定表述\ndeclines in their risk of early __(27)__ 描述\"早死风险的下降\"，early mortality（早死）\n是公共卫生研究的标准术语；第二段后文又提 reductions in their incidence of __(29)__\ndiseases 谈疾病发病率，整段就是\"死亡风险 + 疾病风险\"双线索。\n【竞争词】A) adolescents early adolescents 与 risk of 不构成有意义搭配\nadolescents 是\"青少年\"，与 risk of 搭配不出\"早期青少年的风险\"这种话题；且 adolescents\n留给第 35 题 when they are children or __(35)__ 与 children 并列才贴。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·形容词 early 后接名词作 of 短"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adolescents", "B) arbitrarily", "C) chronic", "D) cognitive", "E) concede", "F) disregard", "G) drop", "H) ever", "I) maneuver", "J) mortality", "K) particularly", "L) patterns", "M) refute", "N) stereotype", "O) vocal"], "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G) drop\n名词\n【词性槽】This was a __ of 32% to 35%，空是单数名词作表语\n【依据】drop of 32% to 35% = \"32% 到 35% 的下降\"\n上文 declines in their risk of early __(27)__ 用到 declines（下降）；本句 This was a\n__(28)__ of 32% to 35% 的 This 就指代那个 decline，drop 与 decline 同义对接\"下降\n幅度\"。\n【竞争词】I) maneuver maneuver 是\"操纵/策略\"，不与百分比搭\nmaneuver of 32% to 35% 在英语里讲不通——maneuver 不表示数量增减，纯属词性对、语\n义偏的干扰。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·This was a __ of 32%"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adolescents", "B) arbitrarily", "C) chronic", "D) cognitive", "E) concede", "F) disregard", "G) drop", "H) ever", "I) maneuver", "J) mortality", "K) particularly", "L) patterns", "M) refute", "N) stereotype", "O) vocal"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) chronic\n形容词\n【词性槽】名词 diseases 前缺一个定语形容词\n【依据】such as heart disease and cancer 是 chronic diseases 的典型例证\n原文 reductions in their incidence of __(29)__ diseases, such as heart disease and\ncancer——心脏病与癌症都是医学上的典型\"慢性病\"（chronic diseases）。such as 后的\n例子直接锁死形容词的语义指向。\n【竞争词】D) cognitive cognitive diseases 不与 heart disease/cancer 类配\ncognitive 指\"认知的\"，cognitive diseases 通常指阿尔茨海默等认知障碍；与 such as 后举出\n的 heart disease and cancer 不属同一范畴。且 cognitive 留给第 32 题 better __(32)__\nfunctioning 与 functioning 搭配是\"认知功能\"的固定表达。\nO) vocal vocal diseases 搭配不成立\nvocal 指\"直言的/嗓音的\"，与心脏病、癌症完全无关，纯属词性对、语义不搭的干扰。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·名词 diseases 前缺一个定语形容"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adolescents", "B) arbitrarily", "C) chronic", "D) cognitive", "E) concede", "F) disregard", "G) drop", "H) ever", "I) maneuver", "J) mortality", "K) particularly", "L) patterns", "M) refute", "N) stereotype", "O) vocal"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) concede\n动词\n【词性槽】主语 The authors + __ + that 宾语从句\n【依据】concede 承认\"研究结论可能被误读\"这一让步语气\n上下文 The authors of the study __(30)__ that some might interpret the study's\nfindings to mean that they can delay starting an exercise program but this would be\na mistake. 是研究作者主动承认\"有人可能会误读\"——这是不利于研究主张的让步，再用\nbut this would be a mistake 反驳。concede（承认不利事实）正是这种\"先让步、后反\n驳\"的标志性动词。\n【竞争词】M) refute refute 与 that 后内容方向相反\nrefute 是\"反驳\"——若作者反驳了\"some might interpret\"这件事，后面就不会再出现 but this\nwould be a mistake（反驳的反驳），逻辑跑偏；且 refute 的宾语通常是论点本身，而非\"某些\n人可能这样理解\"这一描述性事实。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 M) —— refute 与 concede 都能接 that 从句，最易反向。考生若\n忽略后半句 but this would be a mistake 的反驳信号，就可能错选 refute；防坑做法是先\n把整句的 but…mistake 看完，确认\"先让步后反驳\"的链条，再回头锁定 concede。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·主语 The authors + __"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adolescents", "B) arbitrarily", "C) chronic", "D) cognitive", "E) concede", "F) disregard", "G) drop", "H) ever", "I) maneuver", "J) mortality", "K) particularly", "L) patterns", "M) refute", "N) stereotype", "O) vocal"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) disregard\n动词\n【词性槽】should not + 动词原形\n【依据】disregard the benefits = 忽视这些好处\nThere are other benefits, both mental and physical, to staying consistently active\nthat we should not __(31)__.——\"还有其他身心益处是我们不应当 ___ 的\"，逻辑要的\n是\"忽视\"；disregard the benefits 是惯用搭配，且与下文 These real health benefits\ninclude better cognitive functioning and lowered risk of gaining weight. 顺承——正\n因为不应忽视，所以列举出来。\n【竞争词】E) concede concede 是\"承认\"，与\"不应\"组合后逻辑反\nconcede the benefits = \"承认好处\"，should not concede 变成\"不应承认\"，与作者鼓励大家\n重视好处的态度相悖；且 concede 在本文已被第 30 题占用是动词中性候选，留给第 30 更贴。\nM) refute refute the benefits 搭配勉强、语义反\nrefute 是\"反驳（观点/论断）\"，宾语通常是 claim/argument/theory；refute the benefits\n（反驳益处）既不常见，逻辑上 should not refute 也偏向辩论场景，与本句\"忽视\"含义不合。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 E) —— concede 与 disregard 都是动词原形、且都接抽象宾语，\n最易混。other benefits…that we should not __(31)__ 中考生若没注意到下文紧接着列举\n具体益处（better cognitive functioning and lowered risk of gaining weight），就可能\n误填 concede（\"不应承认\"），从而把作者的态度搞反；防坑做法是把空填后整段读一遍\n——作者要肯定益处，对应的动词必为\"忽视\"类。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·should not + 动词原形"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adolescents", "B) arbitrarily", "C) chronic", "D) cognitive", "E) concede", "F) disregard", "G) drop", "H) ever", "I) maneuver", "J) mortality", "K) particularly", "L) patterns", "M) refute", "N) stereotype", "O) vocal"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) cognitive\n形容词\n【词性槽】比较级 better 后接\"形容词 + 名词\"，空是定语形容词\n【依据】cognitive functioning 是\"认知功能\"的固定搭配\nbetter __(32)__ functioning and lowered risk of gaining weight 列举运动的真实健康好\n处——\"更好的认知功能\"与\"降低增重风险\"并列；cognitive functioning 是医学/心理学常\n用术语，正合此处。\n【竞争词】O) vocal vocal functioning 不构成健康层面表述\nvocal 指\"嗓音的、直言的\"，与运动带来的整体健康收益毫无逻辑关联，留作干扰。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·比较级 better 后接\"形容词 +"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adolescents", "B) arbitrarily", "C) chronic", "D) cognitive", "E) concede", "F) disregard", "G) drop", "H) ever", "I) maneuver", "J) mortality", "K) particularly", "L) patterns", "M) refute", "N) stereotype", "O) vocal"], "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K) particularly\n副词\n【词性槽】is + __ + 形容词，副词修饰形容词\n【依据】given the global obesity epidemic 提供了\"尤其\"的现实背景\nThe latter is __(33)__ important given the global obesity epidemic.——\"鉴于全球肥\n胖流行病\"这个背景子句直接为前面提供\"尤其重要\"的理由；particularly 与背景子句一拍\n即合。\n【竞争词】B) arbitrarily arbitrarily important 语义矛盾\narbitrarily 指\"任意地、武断地\"，与 important 搭配会变成\"武断地重要\"，逻辑相悖；六级阅读\n里副词修饰形容词，意思要相互配合而非矛盾。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·is + __ + 形容词，副词修饰形容"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adolescents", "B) arbitrarily", "C) chronic", "D) cognitive", "E) concede", "F) disregard", "G) drop", "H) ever", "I) maneuver", "J) mortality", "K) particularly", "L) patterns", "M) refute", "N) stereotype", "O) vocal"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) patterns\n名词\n【词性槽】those + __ 是名词复数作宾语\n【依据】those patterns 回指前句的 \"make a habit of exercising\"\nif you make a habit of exercising when you are young, you are more likely to keep\nup those __(34)__ later in life. 中 those 指代前面的 \"运动这种习惯\"，patterns（模式/习\n惯）正好对接 habit；keep up patterns（保持这些模式/习惯）也是日常搭配。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·those + __ 是名词复数作宾语"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adolescents", "B) arbitrarily", "C) chronic", "D) cognitive", "E) concede", "F) disregard", "G) drop", "H) ever", "I) maneuver", "J) mortality", "K) particularly", "L) patterns", "M) refute", "N) stereotype", "O) vocal"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) adolescents\n名词\n【词性槽】or 并列 children 的另一个复数名词\n【依据】adolescents 与 children 并列指\"儿童或青少年\"\nstart exercising when they are children or __(35)__——专家建议越早开始越好，最自然\n的并列就是\"儿童或青少年\"；adolescents 在年龄段上紧邻 children，词义与并列结构都\n吻合。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·or 并列 children 的另一个复"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "It is now possible to fully automate a burger restaurant with little human supervision.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】全自动汉堡店＋几乎无人监管\n【定位】Fully automated burger restaurants managed with minimal human oversight — where\ncustomers order at screens, pay electronically, and eat food cooked and delivered by robots —\nare now a possibility.\n几乎无需人工监管的全自动汉堡店——顾客在屏幕上点单、电子支付、吃由机器人烹饪和配\n送的食物——如今已成为可能。\n【改写】It is now possible to fully automate a burger restaurant ↔\nFully automated burger restaurants … are now a possibility\n名词转形容词的句式重构\nwith little human supervision ↔ managed with minimal human oversight\n近义词成对替换\n【辨邻】 D 该段重心是 further automation seems inevitable 的因由，没有题干要的\"burger\nrestaurant＋无人监管\"组合。\nIn an industry keen to slash labor costs and increase profits, further automation seems inevitable", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "At a robot-powered US company, complicated tasks still have to be performed by humans.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】机器人公司里复杂活仍靠人\n【定位】At the robot-powered US pizza-delivery company Zume, the more difficult jobs, such as\ntopping pizzas, are still done by humans.\n在机器人驱动的美国披萨配送公司 Zume，较难的工作（比如给披萨加配料）仍由人来\n完成。\n【改写】complicated tasks ↔ the more difficult jobs\n同义词替换\nstill have to be performed by humans ↔ are still done by humans\n近义动词替换＋被动保持\n【辨邻】 N 该段是 more demand for people front-of-house，针对的是\"前台需求\"，与题干\"难\n活仍靠人\"不是一回事。\nSelf-order screens mean more demand for people front-of-house", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "More automation appears to be a certainty in industries eager to cut labor costs and raise profits.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】削减人工成本、提高利润\n【定位】In an industry keen to slash labor costs and increase profits, further automation\nseems inevitable.\n在一个急于削减人工成本、提高利润的行业里，进一步的自动化看来是不可避免的。\n【改写】industries eager to cut labor costs and raise profits ↔\nan industry keen to slash labor costs and increase profits\n整组近义词替换\nMore automation appears to be a certainty ↔ further automation seems inevitable\n名词化＋近义替换\n【辨邻】 O 该段重心是 jobs will go in conventional bricks-built restaurants，未陈述题干要\n的\"更多自动化成必然\"。\nthe chance to reduce overheads … by employing fewer people or at lower rates", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "There are often front-page reports of failures in technological innovation of the restaurant industry.", "options": null, "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】餐饮科技失败常上头版\n【定位】Embarrassing failures in restaurant tech frequently hit the headlines.\n餐饮科技领域令人尴尬的失败经常登上新闻头条。\n【改写】front-page reports of failures ↔ failures in restaurant tech frequently hit the headlines\n习语转名词短语\noften ↔ frequently\n频率副词同义", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "Robot-chef Flippy does not fear laborious work, nor does it make any complaints.", "options": null, "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】Flippy 不惧重活也不抱怨\n【定位】Like most chefs, Flippy is not afraid of hard work. … But you won't hear Flippy complain or\nsay anything, in fact.\n和大多数厨师一样，Flippy 不惧辛苦的工作。……但事实上，你听不到 Flippy 抱怨，甚至听\n不到它说任何话。\n【改写】does not fear laborious work ↔ is not afraid of hard work\n近义词成对替换\nnor does it make any complaints ↔ you won't hear Flippy complain or say anything\n动词名词化＋否定保持", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "Digital technology makes it possible for businesses to innovate restaurant models.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】数字技术使餐厅模式创新\n【定位】\"For the first time in a few hundred years, digital is allowing us to create new versions\nof restaurants.\"\n\"几百年来第一次，数字技术正让我们能够创造出餐厅的新形态。\"\n【改写】Digital technology makes it possible for businesses to ↔ digital is allowing us to\n近义动词替换\ninnovate restaurant models ↔ create new versions of restaurants\n同义概括\n【辨邻】 E 该段是 recommend dishes to you，落点在推荐与 upsell，未陈述\"创新餐厅模式\"。\nPersonalisation is seen as key to the appeal of this technology\nK 该段是 Automated at-table ordering and payment is seen as the next\nlandmark，未陈述题干的\"创新餐厅模式\"。\nonline restaurant booking has been the easily-won success of the internet age", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "Fondness for human warmth in catering to customers will give hope to employees who are afraid of losing jobs to robots.", "options": null, "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】人情味给怕失业者希望\n【定位】That attachment to the human art of hospitality will be heartening for staff who see\nautomation as a threat to jobs.\n那种对人性化待客艺术的依恋，会让那些视自动化为饭碗威胁的员工感到宽慰。\n【改写】Fondness for human warmth in catering to customers ↔\nattachment to the human art of hospitality\n近义词替换\ngive hope to employees who are afraid of losing jobs to robots ↔\nheartening for staff who see automation as a threat to jobs\n形容词转动词短语＋同义重述\n【辨邻】 N 该段是 Jobs will change, if not immediately decrease in number，落点在岗位结\n构而非\"人性化待客带来的宽慰\"。\nThe industry principle is that automation will allow it to redeploy staff more effectively", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "There is an acute shortage of experienced chefs in the catering industry and automated cooking is expected to fill the gap.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】厨师严重短缺＋自动化补缺\n【定位】The restaurant industry is currently suffering a severe shortage of skilled chefs — a deficit of\n11,000, according to the skills agency People 1st. \"Automation systems designed to make the\nprocess more idiot-proof are trying to bridge that gap,\" says Stefan Chomka, the editor of\nRestaurant magazine.\n餐饮业目前正遭遇熟练厨师的严重短缺——据技能机构 People 1st 称缺口达 11000 人。\n《Restaurant》杂志编辑 Stefan Chomka 说：\"旨在让流程更傻瓜化的自动化系统正试图\n弥合这一缺口。\"\n【改写】an acute shortage of experienced chefs ↔ a severe shortage of skilled chefs\n成对近义词替换\nautomated cooking is expected to fill the gap ↔\nAutomation systems … are trying to bridge that gap\n近义动词替换\n【辨邻】 N 该段是 more demand for people front-of-house，与题干\"短缺＋补缺\"不是同一\n命题。\nSelf-order screens mean more demand for people front-of-house", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "Over the coming decade, many jobs in traditional restaurants will disappear due to robot delivery.", "options": null, "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】十年内传统餐厅岗位因送餐消失\n【定位】Invariably, over the next 10 years, jobs will go in conventional bricks-built restaurants, not\nleast because automation will boost their rapidly growing rival: delivery.\n无可避免地，未来十年，传统砖砌实体餐厅里的岗位将会消失，尤其因为自动化会助推它们\n快速增长的对手——外卖配送。\n【改写】Over the coming decade, many jobs in traditional restaurants will disappear ↔\nover the next 10 years, jobs will go in conventional bricks-built restaurants\n近义词替换\ndue to robot delivery ↔ automation will boost their rapidly growing rival: delivery\n因果还原\n【辨邻】 P 该段是 that robot fleet could deliver almost instantly，落点在送达速度，无题干\n的\"岗位消失\"。\nRestaurants will prepare food ahead of orders coming in\nN 该段是 Jobs will change, if not immediately decrease in number，与题干\"will\ndisappear\"立场相反。\nJobs will change, if not immediately decrease in number", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "According to an expert in AI and robotics, it may be quite some time before a master robot-chef appears.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】AI 专家称大师机器人厨师尚远\n【定位】\"It'll probably be a very long time before we see a five-star robot-chef,\" says Noel Sharkey,\nprofessor of AI and robotics at the University of Sheffield.\n\"我们要见到五星级机器人厨师，恐怕还得很久很久，\"谢菲尔德大学的 AI 与机器人学教授\nNoel Sharkey 说。\n【改写】an expert in AI and robotics ↔ professor of AI and robotics\n上位词替换\nquite some time before a master robot-chef appears ↔\na very long time before we see a five-star robot-chef\n近义词替换\n【辨邻】 O 该段是 Robot food deliveries are the future，话题为配送，与题干\"机器人厨师尚\n远\"无关。\nRobot food deliveries are the future\nH 该段是 offers an idea of the sophistication to come，与题干\"还要很久\"的判断方向\n不同。\nMoley, which will launch soon, offers an idea of the sophistication to come\nPARAGRAPH MAP · 段落主旨图\n个段落各讲了什么\n逐段一句主旨 + 该段最具区分度的「信息指纹」，是逐句回扫定位的工作台。\nA\n用拟人化方式介绍机器人 Flippy：它在加州 Caliburger 长时间重复劳动、追求绝对一\n致却从不抱怨，实质是一只带 3D 热扫描\"眼睛\"的机械臂，能翻汉堡、炸食、监控食物\n并清理。\n信息指纹 机器人 Flippy 的拟人化登场\nB 交代 Flippy 的出身：由 Cali Group 旗下 Miso Robotics 制造，CEO 自称\"一家碰巧卖\n芝士汉堡的科技公司\"；并引出另一台已服务 4 万食客的送餐机器人 Penny。\n信息指纹 Flippy 的制造商与同类 Penny\nC 对比十年前后：机器人厨师从科幻变为价格可投资的现实，并指出\"几乎无需人工监\n管、顾客自助点单付款、由机器人烹饪配送\"的全自动汉堡店如今已成为可能。\n信息指纹 全自动汉堡店成为可能\nD\n技术已存在、只待低成本整合；在急于削减人工成本、提高利润的行业里，进一步自\n动化看似必然；并举 Penny 带来 28% 销售增长、麦当劳自助点单屏使订单额提升\n30% 为证。\n信息指纹 削减人工成本使自动化成必然\nE 个性化被视为该技术吸引力的关键：登录餐厅 App 后，系统能用你的数据与购买历史\n推荐菜品，甚至结合天气与（能读脸时）你的心情，并可靠地推送加价销售。\n信息指纹 个性化与加价推销\nF Flyt 公司 CEO Tom Weaver 认为技术终将让人摆脱\"一刀切菜单\"、实现个人化选择；\n并借赫尔辛基快闪餐厅 Take-In 指出数字化让我们能创造餐厅的新形态。\n信息指纹 数字化创造餐厅新版本\nG 高端餐厅受自动化冲击更慢更微妙：AI 与机器人专家 Noel Sharkey 称\"出现五星机器\n人厨师还要很久\"，因顶级烹饪需对食材的微妙理解，机器人摆放娇嫩食材会极慢。\n信息指纹 五星机器人厨师尚远\nH 当前机器人功能有限：Penny 需人工装盘，披萨公司 Zume 的较难工序仍由人完成；\n但即将面世的 Moley 机械臂能从生料备菜，预示更高复杂度的到来。\n信息指纹 难活仍靠人 + Moley 预示进阶\nI QSR Automations 提供的厨房技术不那么炫：系统自动把订单分发到各屏幕，告诉厨\n师何时开始做每道菜，使一盘鱼薯豆能同时上齐。\n信息指纹 QSR 订单分发系统\nJ 餐饮业正遭遇熟练厨师严重短缺——缺口达 11000 人；《Restaurant》杂志编辑称，\n旨在让流程更\"傻瓜化\"的自动化系统正试图弥合这一缺口。\n信息指纹 厨师短缺，自动化补缺口\nK 在线订位是互联网时代轻易拿下的成功，桌边自动点餐付款被视为下一个里程碑；在\n此之前早期采用者（如曼彻斯特酒吧主 Andy Smith）可能遇到\"低于预期\"的接受度。\n信息指纹 在线订位与桌边点付里程碑\nL 餐饮科技的尴尬失败常上头条：TGI Fridays 无人机砸伤摄影师、Shake Shack 被迫放\n弃全无现金点单；编辑 Chomka 提醒技术若只解决\"并不存在的问题\"便会落空。\n信息指纹 餐饮科技失败屡上头条\nM\n对人性化待客的依恋会让担心被机器人抢饭碗的员工感到宽慰；工会官员 Dave\nTurnbull 担忧若无保障，好处会绕过低薪工人，呼吁议员确保自动化不只惠及少数\n富人。\n信息指纹 人情味宽慰怕失业的员工\nN 行业宣称自动化能更高效地重新部署员工：工作会改变但未必立刻减少，麦当劳去年\n招了 1000 名新经理，Caliburger 有把员工再培训为机器人工程师的计划。\n信息指纹 自动化重新部署而非裁员\nO 最终吸引大品牌的是靠减少用人或压低工资来削减管理费；未来十年传统实体餐厅的\n岗位将消失，尤其因为自动化会助推其快速增长的对手——外卖配送（机器人/无人机\n送餐）。\n信息指纹 十年内传统餐厅岗位因配送而消失\nP\n结合机器学习预测的订单量与 QSR 的实时烹饪数据，机器人车队几乎能即时送达；\nJust Eat 工程总监预言餐厅将提前备餐，智能助手听到即可十分钟送达——机器人正\n在到来。\n信息指纹 预测备餐 + 几乎即时送达", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What often happens when people are eager to pursue individual success?", "options": ["A) They lack a shoulder to cry on.", "B) They have no soul to confide in.", "C) They cannot find reliable friends.", "D) They ignore their ties with friends."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What often happens when people are eager to pursue individual success? 是 What\n引导的事实性问句，问 \"经常发生什么\"，无 infer/imply/suggest 等推断信号。\n【拆句】What often happens when people are eager to pursue individual success? 主句\nWhat often happens 是 wh- 主语疑问句；when 引导时间状语从句，be eager to do 意\n为 \"急于做某事\"。句意 \"当人们急于追求个人成功时，常常会发生什么？\" 结构平铺直叙，\n无陷阱。\n【定位】However, more often than not, in our eagerness to seek individual success, we lose friends\nalong the way and that has become part of the process.\n然而，多数情况下，在我们急于追求个人成功的过程中，我们沿途失去了朋友，而这已经成\n为这一过程的一部分。\n【选项】A) 答非所问\nThey lack a shoulder to cry on. 把 a shoulder to cry on 这一对友谊功能的比喻直接\n当作 \"人们当下缺什么\" 的结论，但原文这一句是描述 \"许多人对友谊的看法\"，不是 \"人\n们追求个人成功时发生的结果\"。\nB) 答非所问\nThey have no soul to confide in. 与 A 同套路，把 a reliable soul to confide in 这一\n对友谊作用的描绘当作题干所问的 \"结果\"，但原文这是 \"友谊在人们眼中的样子\"，不是\n\"追求成功时发生的事\"。\nC) 偷换概念\nThey cannot find reliable friends. 说 \"找不到可靠朋友\"，但原文 we lose friends 强\n调的是 \"主动放弃/丢失现有朋友\"，与 \"找不到\" 是两种不同动作；原文从未说过 \"找\n不到\"。\nD) ✓ 正确\nwe lose friends along the way ↔ They ignore their ties with friends.\n在四项中，只有 They ignore their ties with friends. 与预判 \"失去/疏远朋友\" 在主谓宾\n三层都吻合，故选 D。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What does the author advise people to do in their pursuit of radical individualism?", "options": ["A) Reexamine its detrimental effects.", "B) Shrug off the loss of their loved ones.", "C) Avoid the chaos and turmoil involved.", "D) Remove aggressive obstacles in the way."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author advise people to do in their pursuit of radical individualism?\n直接问作者建议做什么，是对文中作者表态句的事实性提问，不是推断。\n【拆句】What does the author advise people to do in their pursuit of radical individualism?\nadvise sb. to do sth. = 建议某人做某事；their pursuit of radical individualism 是\n\"his/her/their + 名词 + of\" 的所属结构，等价于 \"when they pursue radical\nindividualism\"。句意：作者建议人们在追求极端个人主义时做什么？\n【定位】We must reevaluate the harm of radical, individual freedom.\n我们必须重新评估极端个人自由的危害。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nreevaluate the harm of radical, individual freedom ↔ Reexamine its detrimental effects.\n在四项中，只有 Reexamine its detrimental effects. 同时覆盖了 \"再审视\" 与 \"危害\" 两\n个语义成分，故选 A。\nB) 与原文相反\nShrug off the loss of their loved ones. 是 we use this excuse to shrug off losing\npeople who we once thought to be loved ones 里被作者批判的 \"错误做法\"，并非\n作者建议。\nC) 无中生有\nAvoid the chaos and turmoil involved. 把 The chaos and never-ending turmoil\nthat accompanies everyday life 这一对生活状态的描述当作作者建议，但原文只是把\n它列为 \"我们丢友谊的借口\"，作者既未说要避免也未说要拥抱。\nD) 偷换概念\nRemove aggressive obstacles in the way. 把 an aggressive obstacle in the pursuit\nof stable friendships 的名词搭配换皮成 \"清除前进路上的强劲障碍\"，但作者要清除的\n不是抽象意义上的 \"前进路上的障碍\"——他要重新评估的是极端个人主义之害。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What is American society's assumption of personal achievement?", "options": ["A) It is to be duly celebrated and highly commended.", "B) It is a sure way to fulfill a person's dream.", "C) It revolves around an individualistic culture.", "D) It reflects a person's exceptional endeavor."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What is American society's assumption of personal achievement? 是 What is 引导的\n事实性问句，问 \"美国社会对个人成就的预设是什么\"，无推断信号词。\n【拆句】What is American society's assumption of personal achievement? American society's\nassumption of personal achievement 是双层所属结构：assumption of personal\nachievement 等于 \"对个人成就的预设\"；前置 American society's 进一步说明这是 \"美国\n社会的\"。句意：美国社会对个人成就的预设是什么？\n【定位】Society tends to celebrate personal achievement and commend it based on the assumption\nthat winning or succeeding is a consequence of working harder than the next person.\n社会倾向于赞美个人成就，并基于一种预设来推崇它，这种预设是：获胜或成功是比别人更\n努力工作的结果。\n【选项】A) 答非所问\nIt is to be duly celebrated and highly commended. 把 Society tends to celebrate\npersonal achievement and commend it 的 \"社会的做法\" 直接当成 \"对个人成就的预\n设\"，但题干问的是 the assumption 这个预设本身的内容，不是社会怎么做。\nB) 无中生有\nIt is a sure way to fulfill a person's dream. 说 \"个人成就是实现梦想的可靠途径\"，原\n文从未将个人成就与 \"实现梦想\" 直接挂钩；原文涉及 dream 只出现在 threatened the\ndream of many Americans 中，且是普及医保的对立面，与个人成就的预设无关。\nC) 偷换概念\nIt revolves around an individualistic culture. 把 Many of us have fallen victim to\nan individualistic culture 的上位归类（属于个人主义文化）当作预设本身，但题干问\n的是 \"对个人成就所持的预设\"，不是 \"个人成就属于哪种文化\"。\nD) ✓ 正确\nwinning or succeeding is a consequence of working harder than the next person ↔\nIt reflects a person's exceptional endeavor.\n四项中只有 It reflects a person's exceptional endeavor. 准确捕捉 \"成功背后是个人非\n凡努力\" 这一预设，故选 D。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What is preached in many success stories?", "options": ["A) Attaching importance to academic gains on the road to success.", "B) Investing one's time in work instead of connecting with friends.", "C) Trying to strengthen friendships in one's pursuit of personal success.", "D) Excelling in academic performance to get into a prestigious college."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What is preached in many success stories? 问的是 \"很多励志故事中宣扬了什么\"，是对\n文中具体表述的事实性追问。\n【拆句】What is preached in many success stories? 主语 What 是宾语提前的 wh-；is preached\n为被动语态，意为 \"被宣扬\"。句意：很多励志故事中宣扬的是什么？句式简单、无陷阱。\n【定位】Motivational speakers will push their audience to be complacent to the inevitable end of\nlosing friends. Spend too much time with friends? You're losing time better utilized on your\nachievements. Invest time in your relationships? Invest time in your work instead.\n励志演讲者会推动听众坦然接受 \"失去朋友\" 这一不可避免的结局。和朋友花太多时间？你\n正在浪费本可用于个人成就的时间。把时间投入到人际关系上？不如把时间投入到工作上。\n【选项】A) 张冠李戴\nAttaching importance to academic gains on the road to success. 来自 Don't\nworry about losing friendships, just focus on study and getting into college，但\n这是 \"高中时听到的话\"（作者的个人经历），紧接其后才提到 \"a sentiment that is not\nunique to my experience. It's preached in many success stories\"——题干问的是\n\"在 many success stories 中被宣扬的\"，即作者随后展开的演讲家观点，不是高中老师\n的话。\nB) ✓ 正确\nInvest time in your relationships? Invest time in your work instead. ↔\nInvesting one's time in work instead of connecting with friends.\n四项中只有 Investing one's time in work instead of connecting with friends. 同时覆\n盖 \"投入工作\" 与 \"放弃朋友联系\" 两层，故选 B。\nC) 与原文相反\nTrying to strengthen friendships in one's pursuit of personal success. 是作者自己\n在 P8 P9 提出的反向建议，不是 success stories 所宣扬的内容；many success\nstories 宣扬的恰恰是放弃友谊。\nD) 以偏概全\nExcelling in academic performance to get into a prestigious college. 同样源自\nfocus on study and getting into college 这句高中老师的话，且把 college 升级为\nprestigious college（名校）属于无中生有；不属于 many success stories 所宣扬的\n内容。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What can we infer from the passage regarding the pursuit of success?", "options": ["A) No one can succeed without pushing limits.", "B) No one can afford to neglect close friends.", "C) Collectivism is superior to individualism.", "D) Success experts often lead people to ruin."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （推断题）\n【判型】What can we infer from the passage regarding the pursuit of success? 中 infer 一词\n直接亮明题型属性。\n【拆句】What can we infer from the passage regarding the pursuit of success? What 作 infer\n的宾语；regarding = 关于，介词短语作后置定语限定 infer 的范围。句意：关于追求成\n功，我们可以从本文推断出什么？句式平铺直叙。\n【定位】We cannot be complacent with losing friendships because they are our anchors. Yes, pursue\nsuccess. Challenge yourself. Push limits. But contrary to all the self-proclaimed success\nexperts, doing it alone does not make you more successful or any better than someone who\nachieved it with the help of others.\n我们不能对失去友谊袖手旁观，因为朋友是我们的锚。是的，去追求成功，挑战自己，突破\n极限。但与所有自封的成功专家所言相反，独自一人完成它，并不会让你更成功，也并不比\n那些借助他人之力成功的人更高一筹。\n【选项】A) 过度推断\nNo one can succeed without pushing limits. 借 Push limits 一词夸大为 \"没有人不突\n破极限就能成功\"，但原文仅说 \"是的，去突破极限\" 作为肯定后退一步的让步话，并未\n声明 \"不突破极限就不能成功\"。\nB) ✓ 正确\nWe cannot be complacent with losing friendships because they are our anchors. ↔\nNo one can afford to neglect close friends.\nhaving people to support and love you on the way is far more important than any end result ↔\nNo one can afford to neglect close friends.\n在四项中，只有 No one can afford to neglect close friends. 是从 P8 P9 两处强表态\n合理延伸一步得到的结论，故选 B。\nC) 过度推断\nCollectivism is superior to individualism. 把 Radical individualism elevates the\nfreedom of the individual over the collective 与 universal healthcare, an example\nof collectivism 加工成 \"集体主义比个人主义优越\" 的全称比较，但原文只反对 \"极端\"\n个人主义，并未抬高集体主义本身。\nD) 过度推断\nSuccess experts often lead people to ruin. 来自 contrary to all the self-\nproclaimed success experts，作者只是 \"与所谓的成功专家相反\"，并未宣称 \"成功专\n家把人引向毁灭\"——ruin 一词是文外强化。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "推断题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What does the author think about today's America?", "options": ["A) It is struggling to transform fiction into reality.", "B) It has been the focus of many vital conversations.", "C) It still has a long way to go before equity can be truly realized.", "D) It has got to a point where people disagree on nearly every issue."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What does the author think about today's America? 问的是作者对一个明确对象的\n具体看法，不是问全文主旨、不含 infer/imply。\n【拆句】句子结构是 What does the author think about today's America?，主语 the author、谓\n语 think、介词宾语 today's America；无从句、无否定，结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】However, by honoring King's legacy, we can get our country back on the path toward\ntrue equity.\n然而，只要传承（马丁·路德·）金的精神遗产，我们就能让国家重新回到通向真正公平的道\n路上。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nstruggling to transform fiction into reality 的字面义是「努力把虚构变成现实」，与\nstruggling to separate truth from fantasy（努力分清真相与幻象）方向相反——原文\n是要分开两者，不是把虚构变成现实。\nB) 张冠李戴\nIt has been the focus of many vital conversations 把美国当成「对话的焦点」；但\nthe battle for equity is at the heart of these vital conversations 里说的是「公平之\n战是这些对话的核心」，焦点是 equity，不是美国。\nC) ✓ 正确\nget our country back on the path toward true equity ↔\nstill has a long way to go before equity can be truly realized\nour society is still struggling to separate truth from fantasy ↔ still has a long way to go\n预判「离真正公平还远」与 still has a long way to go before equity can be truly\nrealized 一一对应，且呼应 still struggling——锁定 C。\nD) 以偏概全\ndisagree on nearly every issue 把分歧泛化到「几乎每一个议题」；原文只举了\nclimate change 与 how slavery is taught 等具体议题，且作者关心的核心是 equity，\n没有「几乎每个议题都有分歧」的论断。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What do we learn from the passage about black students in the past 20 years?", "options": ["A) They have been unfairly treated regarding higher education.", "B) They have been battling hard to address equity issues.", "C) They have boosted their lifetime incomes by billions of dollars.", "D) They have come to see the consequences of racial discrimination."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What do we learn from the passage about black students in the past 20 years?\n问从文中「得知」什么，明确锁定群体加时间，是要求回原文复述事实。\n【拆句】What do we learn from the passage about black students in the past 20 years? 是\nWhat...about... 结构，about 后跟的 black students in the past 20 years 是定位对象；结\n构清晰，无陷阱。\n【定位】A 2020 report by Citi indicated that \"Black students could have increased their lifetime\nincomes $90 – $113 billion\" if equity issues related to higher education had been adequately\naddressed over the past two decades.\n花旗集团 2020 年的一份报告指出：如果过去二十年里高等教育中的公平问题得到了妥善处\n理，黑人学生本可以多挣 900 亿到 1130 亿美元的终身收入。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nif equity issues related to higher education had been adequately addressed over the past two\ndecades\n↔ unfairly treated regarding higher education\nBlack students could have increased their lifetime incomes $90 – $113 billion ↔\nThey have been unfairly treated\n预判「过去二十年高等教育对黑人学生不公」与 unfairly treated regarding higher\neducation 一一对应——锁定 A。\nB) 无中生有\nbattling hard to address equity issues 把「被动遭遇不公」换成「主动奋力解决公平\n问题」；原文只说公平问题如果被妥善处理黑人学生本可多挣钱，并未说他们自己在奋\n斗解决。\nC) 与原文相反\nThey have boosted their lifetime incomes by billions of dollars 用现在完成时陈述\n「已经多挣了」；原文是 could have increased 虚拟语气，含义是「本可以但实际没\n有」——选项把虚拟当作了现实。\nD) 偷换概念\ncome to see the consequences of racial discrimination 把话题偷换成「看清种族歧\n视的后果」；原文谈的是高等教育公平问题与黑人学生的收入损失，并未提及他们「认\n识到歧视后果」这一心理变化。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "How can Americans bridge the differences that divide them?", "options": ["A) By dealing with the thorny issues confronting them.", "B) By seizing every chance to enhance racial harmony.", "C) By continuing to pursue Martin Luther King Jr.'s cause.", "D) By striving to identify exceedingly valuable opportunities."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】题干 How can Americans bridge the differences that divide them? 问的是「如何做到\nX」，要求从原文找具体路径，是细节题。\n【拆句】How can Americans bridge the differences that divide them? 中 that divide them 是\n定语从句修饰 differences，等于 the differences which divide Americans；结构稍长，\n但无否定无比较，逻辑直白。\n【定位】However, by honoring King's legacy, we can get our country back on the path toward\ntrue equity.\n然而，只要传承（马丁·路德·）金的精神遗产，我们就能让国家重新回到通向真正公平的道\n路上。\n【选项】A) 答非所问\ndealing with the thorny issues confronting them 描述的是「直面棘手问题」。原文\naddress our shared issues head-on 这一表述是作者对 polarization 的回应，并非他\n给出的「弥合分歧」路径；定位句 P4 给出的路径只有 honoring King's legacy。\nB) 无中生有\nseizing every chance to enhance racial harmony 把答案拉到「抓住一切机会促进种\n族和谐」。原文只谈 equity、bridge differences、honor King's legacy，没有 racial\nharmony 的提法，也没有 every chance 这种全称量化。\nC) ✓ 正确\nby honoring King's legacy ↔ continuing to pursue Martin Luther King Jr.'s cause\nin the words of Martin Luther King Jr., \"A society at peace with itself, a society that can live\nwith its conscience.\"\n↔ Martin Luther King Jr.'s cause\n预判「继承金的精神遗产」与 By continuing to pursue Martin Luther King Jr.'s\ncause 一一对应——锁定 C。\nD) 偷换概念\nstriving to identify exceedingly valuable opportunities 截取了 missing out on\nvaluable opportunities 中的 valuable opportunities，但原文的语义是「我们正在错\n失机会」，并非「努力识别极有价值的机会」；选项把「错过」扭成「努力寻找」。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "How do most Americans view America as a nation according to a recent survey?", "options": ["A) It is compelled to address its historical social issues.", "B) It is delaying advancements toward racial equality.", "C) It has kept itself from polarization.", "D) It has become increasingly split."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】题干 How do most Americans view America as a nation according to a recent\nsurvey? 明确说 according to a recent survey，要求复述调查数据，是细节题。\n【拆句】How do most Americans view America as a nation according to a recent survey? 主\n干是 How do most Americans view America，as a nation 是补足语相当于把 America\n视作一个国家整体，according to a recent survey 是状语限定信息来源；无陷阱。\n【定位】A recent survey found that 77 percent of Americans believe the nation is \"more divided than\never before\".\n一项近期调查发现，77% 的美国人认为这个国家「前所未有地分裂」。\n【选项】A) 张冠李戴\ncompelled to address its historical social issues 借用了 they should compel us to\naddress our shared issues head-on 的措辞，但那句话是作者对 polarization 报告的\n回应，不是 most Americans 在调查中表达的看法；本题问的是 most Americans 的\n看法。\nB) 张冠李戴\ndelaying advancements toward racial equality 借用 our deeply rooted divisions\nare delaying these advancements，但原文里拖延进步的主语是 our deeply rooted\ndivisions（根深蒂固的分裂），且这是作者的判断而非调查结论；本题问的是调查里美\n国人怎么看美国。\nC) 与原文相反\nhas kept itself from polarization 意为「避免了两极分化」。原文 reports of\nincreased polarization 明确说「两极分化在加剧」，且 77% 的美国人认为国家更分裂\n——选项与调查结论方向相反。\nD) ✓ 正确\nmore divided than ever before ↔ increasingly split\n77 percent of Americans believe ↔ It has become increasingly split\n预判「国家越来越分裂」与 has become increasingly split 一一对应——锁定 D。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-1-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What does the author suggest Americans do?", "options": ["A) Ensure the well-being of their children and grandchildren.", "B) Make joint efforts to tackle the nation's equity issues.", "C) Restrain their intolerance and discrimination.", "D) Restore social order for a harmonious nation."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What does the author suggest Americans do? 问作者建议美国人做什么——是一\n个落到具体动作的细节题，不是问全文中心。\n【拆句】What does the author suggest Americans do? 是宾语从句结构，suggest 后省略\nthat，Americans 是从句主语、do 是从句谓语原形；结构清晰，无陷阱。\n【定位】On this Martin Luther King Day and beyond, let's commit to working together to disrupt the\nstatus quo.\n在马丁·路德·金日及今后，让我们一起承诺携手努力，打破现状。\n【选项】A) 答非所问\nEnsure the well-being of their children and grandchildren 借用 our children and\nour grandchildren deserve better，但那句是说「下一代值得更好」——是作者的忧\n虑动机，不是他给读者的具体建议；选项把动机当成了行动。\nB) ✓ 正确\nlet's commit to working together to disrupt the status quo ↔\nMake joint efforts to tackle the nation's equity issues\nthe path toward true equity ↔ the nation's equity issues\n预判「联手打破现状」与 Make joint efforts to tackle the nation's equity issues 一一\n对应——锁定 B。\nC) 偷换概念\nRestrain their intolerance and discrimination 把答案换成「克制偏狭与歧视」。原文\nUnless we get honest about intolerance, bias 要求的是「对偏狭与偏见保持诚实」\n——是承认与谈论，而不是 restrain（克制压抑）。\nD) 偷换概念\nRestore social order for a harmonious nation 借用末段 build a better, more\ninclusive nation 的「建设国家」色彩，但 restore social order（恢复社会秩序）原文\n从未提及；原文是「建设更包容」，不是「恢复秩序」。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}]});
